window.JTS = window.JTS || {}; JTS.data = JTS.data || {};

JTS.data.programme.teach = {
  "U1": {
    lead: "<p>These questions appear as “Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word?” or “As used in the text, what does the word X most nearly mean?” They are among the most common R&amp;W questions. They reward a careful reading method more than a huge vocabulary.</p>",
    parts: [
      {"title": "Part 1 · The core idea", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<p>The meaning of a word depends on the sentence it lives in. Take “run”. In “she will run the marathon” it means race. In “he will run the company” it means manage. In “there's a run in my stocking” it means a tear. The word did not change; the context did.</p>\n<p>So the question is never “which word do I know?” It is “what is this sentence saying, and which word fits that meaning?”</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 2 · The four-step method", "mins": "about 6 min", "html": "<ol>\n<li><b>Read past the blank.</b> Read the whole sentence, and ideally the one before and after. The clue is often one sentence away.</li>\n<li><b>Find the clue.</b> It is usually one of four kinds:\n  <ul>\n  <li><i>Contrast:</i> “The results were not disappointing; in fact, they were ___” needs a positive word.</li>\n  <li><i>Example or description:</i> the sentence describes the situation in plain words, and the blank is a precise word for it.</li>\n  <li><i>Consequence:</i> “Because the bridge collapsed, the engineer's reputation was ___” needs a word about damage.</li>\n  <li><i>Comparison:</i> “Like her earlier work, the new novel is ___” matches what was said about the earlier work.</li>\n  </ul></li>\n<li><b>Predict your own word.</b> Before looking at the choices, put a simple word in the blank: “different”, “careless”, “improve”, “harm”. This is the strongest move in the method. The choices become a matching exercise, not a vocabulary quiz.</li>\n<li><b>Test each choice in the sentence.</b> Plug it in and ask: does the sentence still say exactly what the passage said? If it changes the meaning, it is wrong here, however impressive the word.</li>\n</ol>"},
      {"title": "Part 3 · Worked example", "mins": "about 5 min", "html": "<blockquote>Early critics dismissed the painter's work as derivative, but decades later, art historians celebrated the ___ qualities that set her apart from her contemporaries.\n<ol class=\"lw-opts\"><li><b>A</b> conventional</li><li><b>B</b> divergent</li><li><b>C</b> meticulous</li><li><b>D</b> prolific</li></ol></blockquote>\n<p>The clue is “set her apart from her contemporaries”, so the blank means something like “distinctive” or “different”. <b>Divergent</b> means different, heading in a separate direction: it matches. “Conventional” is the opposite. “Meticulous” (very careful) and “prolific” (producing a lot) could both be true of a painter, but the sentence is not about care or quantity. The answer is B, and we reached it from the clue, not from knowing “divergent” cold.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 4 · Two special cases", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<p><b>Familiar word, unusual meaning.</b> “Tame” usually means domesticated, but in a sentence about someone controlling their emotions it means to bring under control. On “as used in the text” questions, plug in your everyday meaning. If the sentence stops making sense, find the meaning that does fit.</p>\n<p><b>The familiarity trap.</b> A word you recognize feels correct and a word you have never seen feels wrong. Neither feeling is evidence. Several wrong choices are often negative when the right answer is also negative, so “it sounds negative” settles nothing.</p>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-tip\"><b>Tip.</b> If two choices seem to fit, go back and sharpen the clue. The SAT builds one distractor that is close but slightly off in meaning or precision. If you cannot decide, you have not found the exact clue yet.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 5 · Word parts as a backup tool", "mins": "", "html": "<p>Use word parts when a choice is unfamiliar, and always finish with the plug-it-into-the-sentence test. A word part gives the general direction of a meaning; only the context gives the exact one.</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Prefix</th><th>Signals</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>un-, in-, im-, il-, dis-, non-</td><td>not, opposite</td><td>unyielding, impartial, illegible</td></tr>\n<tr><td>re-</td><td>again, back</td><td>reinforce, retract</td></tr>\n<tr><td>pre-, fore-</td><td>before</td><td>precursor</td></tr>\n<tr><td>post-</td><td>after</td><td>posthumous</td></tr>\n<tr><td>sub-, super-, hyper-</td><td>under, above, beyond</td><td>subordinate</td></tr>\n<tr><td>inter-, trans-</td><td>between, across</td><td>interdependent, transmit</td></tr>\n<tr><td>mono-, bi-, poly-</td><td>one, two, many</td><td>monotony</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Suffix</th><th>Makes</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>-tion, -ment, -ness, -ity</td><td>nouns</td><td>preservation, assessment, eagerness, plausibility</td></tr>\n<tr><td>-ify, -ize, -ate</td><td>verbs</td><td>clarify, emphasize</td></tr>\n<tr><td>-ous, -ful, -ive, -able, -ish</td><td>adjectives</td><td>precarious, inventive, manageable</td></tr>\n<tr><td>-ly</td><td>usually adverbs</td><td>cautiously (but: friendly, likely are adjectives)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Root</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>ben- / mal-</td><td>good / bad</td><td>beneficial, malevolent</td></tr>\n<tr><td>dict</td><td>say, tell</td><td>predict, contradict</td></tr>\n<tr><td>spect</td><td>look</td><td>inspect, retrospect, circumspect (cautious)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>cred</td><td>believe</td><td>credible, credulous (too ready to believe)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>loqu / loc</td><td>speak</td><td>eloquent, loquacious (talkative)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>chron</td><td>time</td><td>chronological, anachronistic</td></tr>\n<tr><td>anthrop</td><td>human</td><td>anthropology, misanthrope</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ambi- / amphi-</td><td>both, around</td><td>ambivalent (pulled both ways)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Example.</b> A choice is “loquacious” and the clue says the scientist “rarely used more words than necessary”. The root <i>loqu</i> (speak) tells you the word is about talking, so it is worth testing. Plug it in: does “talkative” fit a scientist who rarely speaks? No, it reverses the idea, so reject it. The root gets you to the right neighbourhood; the clue makes the decision.</p>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-mistake\"><b>Common mistake.</b> Never let a word part decide on its own. “Inflammable” looks like “not flammable” but means flammable. “Nonplussed” looks like calm but means confused. And not every look-alike shares a root: “laconic” (using few words) comes from Laconia, not from <i>loqu</i>.</p>\n<p><b>Word-form questions.</b> When the choices are forms of one word (differ, different, difference, differently), decide what the blank must be grammatically: a thing, an action, or a description. Then the suffix decides.</p>"}
    ]
  },
  "U2": {
    lead: "<p>These questions ask one of three things: “What is the main purpose of the text?”, “How is the text structured?”, or “What function does this sentence serve?” All three test one skill: seeing the passage as a machine where each part does a job, not as a pile of sentences.</p>",
    parts: [
      {"title": "Part 1 · The core idea", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<p>Every text was written to accomplish something: argue for a position, explain how something works, describe a scene, compare two things, or correct a misconception. That goal is the <b>purpose</b>. The <b>structure</b> is the order the author chose to reach it. The <b>function</b> of a sentence is the job that one sentence does inside the plan.</p>\n<p>What a sentence <i>says</i> and what it <i>does</i> are different things. Take “For example, in 1997, rainfall in the region dropped by half.” It says a fact about rain in 1997. It does something else: it gives an example supporting the claim before it. Function questions test the <i>does</i>. A choice that only repeats the content (“describes rainfall patterns”) is probably wrong.</p>\n<p>A film scene works the same way. Two characters may talk about the weather, but the scene is there to reveal tension or introduce a conflict.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 2 · The job-label method", "mins": "about 6 min", "html": "<ol>\n<li><b>Label each chunk with its job</b>, in ordinary words. Ask “why is this part here?” Common labels:\n  <ul>\n  <li>Sets the scene or gives background</li>\n  <li>Introduces a problem or question</li>\n  <li>Makes a claim</li>\n  <li>Gives a reason or explanation</li>\n  <li>Gives an example</li>\n  <li>Reports a result or evidence</li>\n  <li>Adds a qualification or counterpoint (“however, some critics disagree”)</li>\n  <li>Draws a conclusion</li>\n  </ul></li>\n<li><b>Describe the sequence.</b> For example: “describes the setting → explains her decision → describes her personality.” That one-line map answers any structure or purpose question about the text.</li>\n<li><b>For an underlined sentence, put it back on the map.</b> Ask what it adds to what came before and what it sets up for what follows. Never judge the sentence in isolation.</li>\n</ol>"},
      {"title": "Part 3 · The three question types", "mins": "about 5 min", "html": "<p><b>Main purpose.</b> The answer is the one-sentence version of your map: “The text explains why X happened.” Wrong choices have predictable shapes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><i>Too narrow:</i> describes one detail, not the whole purpose.</li>\n<li><i>Invented attitude:</i> the passage was neutral, but the choice says the author “criticizes” or “celebrates”.</li>\n<li><i>Too vague:</i> “develops the argument” says nothing. A real answer shows how: “presents a counterexample to the claim.”</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Overall structure.</b> These describe how the whole text unfolds: “makes a claim, then presents evidence supporting it.” Test every part of the choice, not just the part that looks right. Wrong structure choices are usually 80% right and 20% wrong, and one mismatch is enough to reject the whole choice.</p>\n<p><b>Function of a sentence.</b> State its job in your own plain words first: “this gives an example of the claim.” Then match. Translate abstract choices (“it underscores a limitation of a methodology”) into plain words (“it points out a problem with how the study was done”) before deciding.</p>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-tip\"><b>Tip.</b> When a choice has several phrases, mark each one true or false against your map. The right choice is all true; the trap is mostly true with one wrong phrase. Students lose these by matching the first phrase and skimming the rest.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 4 · Worked example", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<blockquote>(1) In the 1920s, most biologists assumed that genes were fixed and unchanging. (2) However, Barbara McClintock's experiments with maize suggested that some genes could move within a chromosome. (3) Her findings were met with skepticism for decades. (4) Today, her discoveries are considered foundational to modern genetics.</blockquote>\n<p><b>Map.</b> (1) the old assumption, as background → (2) her finding against it → (3) the initial resistance → (4) the eventual recognition.</p>\n<p><b>Main purpose.</b> “Describes a scientific idea whose significance was not recognized until later.” Not “argues that biologists should be more open-minded”: the author gives no advice, so that is an invented attitude. Not “explains how genes move within chromosomes”: too narrow, and the passage tells her story without explaining the biology.</p>\n<p><b>Structure.</b> “Presents a widely held assumption, describes a finding that challenged it, then traces how attitudes toward the finding changed.” The map is the answer.</p>\n<p><b>Function of sentence 3.</b> It qualifies sentence 2 by showing the finding was not accepted at once, and it sets up the contrast in sentence 4. A choice saying “it explains why McClintock's experiments were flawed” invents a criticism the text never makes.</p>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-mistake\"><b>Common mistake.</b> Reading text order as time order. A passage can go 1990s → 2000s → 1980s. Call a structure chronological only if the passage is organized around the passage of time.</p>"}
    ]
  },
  "U3": {
    lead: "<p>Your first Math class. These questions are the backbone of the Algebra domain. They show up as “what is the value of <i>x</i>?”, “which equation represents this situation?”, and inequality word problems. One of the most reliable Desmos shortcuts in the course lives here too.</p>",
    parts: [
      {"title": "Part 1 · The core idea", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<p>A linear equation is any equation where the unknown appears to the first power: no <i>x</i>², no √<i>x</i>, no 1/<i>x</i>. Examples: 3<i>x</i> + 7 = 22 or 5(<i>x</i> − 2) = 3<i>x</i> + 4.</p>\n<p>Solving is one idea: <b>undo what was done to <i>x</i>, in reverse order, doing the same thing to both sides.</b> The equals sign is a promise that both sides weigh the same. If <i>x</i> was multiplied by 3, divide by 3. If 7 was added, subtract 7. Everything else is organization.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 2 · The solving sequence", "mins": "about 6 min", "html": "<ol>\n<li><b>Clear fractions and parentheses.</b> Multiply both sides by the common denominator and distribute any parentheses. Messy equations are usually clean equations in disguise.</li>\n<li><b>Collect all <i>x</i> terms on one side.</b> Move every <i>x</i> term to one side and every number to the other. A term that crosses the equals sign changes its sign.</li>\n<li><b>Combine and divide.</b> Combine like terms, then divide by the coefficient of <i>x</i>.</li>\n</ol>\n<blockquote><b>Example.</b> Solve <i>x</i>/3 + 5 = <i>x</i>/2 − 1.\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Step 1: multiply everything by 6 → 2x + 30 = 3x − 6</div><div>Step 2: subtract 2x from both sides → 30 = x − 6</div><div>Step 3: add 6 → x = 36</div><div>Check: 36/3 + 5 = 17 and 36/2 − 1 = 17</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>The check takes 10 seconds and catches almost every sign error.</p>\n<p><b>Inequalities</b> work the same way with one extra rule: when you multiply or divide both sides by a negative number, the sign flips. −2<i>x</i> &gt; 10 becomes <i>x</i> &lt; −5. Forgetting the flip is the most common error in this topic. Also read the question carefully: the SAT sometimes asks which <i>value</i> is in the solution set, so test each choice in your solved inequality.</p>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-mistake\"><b>Common mistake.</b> Sign errors when moving terms across the equals sign. 5<i>x</i> + 3 = 2<i>x</i> + 11 becomes 5<i>x</i> − 2<i>x</i> = 11 − 3, not 11 + 3: the +3 that crossed becomes −3 on the right. Write the intermediate step down instead of doing it in your head.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 3 · The two special answers", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<p>Sometimes simplifying removes <i>x</i> entirely, and the result tells you something.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>No solution:</b> you end with a false statement. 2(<i>x</i> + 3) = 2<i>x</i> + 10 simplifies to 2<i>x</i> + 6 = 2<i>x</i> + 10, then 6 = 10, which is never true. No value of <i>x</i> works.</li>\n<li><b>Infinitely many solutions:</b> you end with a true statement. 2(<i>x</i> + 3) = 2<i>x</i> + 6 simplifies to 6 = 6. Every value of <i>x</i> works, because both sides were the same expression.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The SAT often asks “how many solutions does the equation have?” If both sides say the same thing in different clothes, it is infinitely many. If the clothing hides a contradiction, it is no solution.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 4 · Word problems", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<p>Word problems here are translation exercises. Learn the dictionary:</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Words</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>is</td><td>=</td></tr>\n<tr><td>more than, sum</td><td>add</td></tr>\n<tr><td>less than</td><td>subtract, in the order said: “5 less than <i>x</i>” is <i>x</i> − 5, not 5 − <i>x</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>per</td><td>divide or multiply, depending on the setup</td></tr>\n<tr><td>at least</td><td>≥</td></tr>\n<tr><td>at most</td><td>≤</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Two traps. “Less than” flips the order, and it is the most-tested translation trap. And some questions ask for an expression, not a solution: after translating, stop. Do not solve something that is not asked.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 5 · The Desmos shortcut", "mins": "about 2 min", "html": "<p>For a “what is the value of <i>x</i>” question, type the left side into Desmos as one expression and the right side as another. Desmos draws two lines and puts a dot where they cross. Click the dot: its <i>x</i>-coordinate is the answer. Ten seconds, no sign errors.</p>\n<p>Use algebra for “how many solutions” questions, since they ask about the structure of the equation. Desmos is the visual check there: parallel lines mean no solution, identical lines mean infinitely many.</p>"}
    ]
  },
  "U4": {
    lead: "<p>You get two short texts on the same topic, then a question like “How would the author of Text 2 respond to the claim in Text 1?” or “Which choice best describes the relationship between the two texts?” It is easy to get each text roughly right and still miss the question, because the wrong answer usually describes one text correctly and the other one wrong.</p>",
    parts: [
      {"title": "Part 1 · The core idea", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<p>The relationship between two texts is one of a small set of moves:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Agreement:</b> Text 2 supports or endorses Text 1's claim.</li>\n<li><b>Disagreement:</b> Text 2 rejects or challenges Text 1's claim.</li>\n<li><b>Qualification:</b> Text 2 agrees but adds a limit or complication (“true, but only under certain conditions”).</li>\n<li><b>Accepts the evidence, disputes the explanation:</b> “those findings are real, but you're interpreting them wrong.”</li>\n<li><b>Extending or applying:</b> Text 2 builds on Text 1 and takes it to a new case.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>A qualification is not a rejection.</b> If Text 2 says the finding holds but has limits, that is agreement with a caveat. Students see “however” in Text 2 and pick “Text 2 disputes Text 1”, and the test punishes that reflex. Read what the second half of the sentence does.</p>\n<p>The mirror-image rule: <b>a shared topic does not imply agreement.</b> Two texts can both be about bird deterrence and hold opposite views. Decide the relationship from the claims, never from the topic.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 2 · The method", "mins": "about 6 min", "html": "<ol>\n<li><b>Write a one-sentence note on each text, separately.</b> “Text 1 claims ___. Text 2 claims ___.” Note what each claims and why. If names confuse you, use “Text 1's author” and “Text 2's author”.</li>\n<li><b>Check the direction of the question.</b> “How would Text 2's author respond to Text 1?” is not “How would Text 1's author respond to Text 2?” The choices are built to punish swapping the two. Mark who is responding to whom before you read the choices.</li>\n<li><b>Match your relationship word to the choices.</b> The choices usually differ by relationship word: “undermines”, “supports”, “provides context for”, “qualifies”. Pick the one your notes support, not the one whose details sound familiar.</li>\n</ol>\n<blockquote><b>Example of step 1.</b> Text 1: an early trial of drones for scaring birds from vineyards showed that drones reduce bird damage. Text 2: newer methods target specific birds, not whole flocks.\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Text 1 = “drones work, generally”</div><div>Text 2 = “a better approach is targeted deterrence”</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>This is not disagreement. Text 2 builds on the idea and points to an improvement, so the answer is something like “Text 2 acknowledges a useful start but suggests a more refined approach.” Wrong choices would say “completely ineffective” (too extreme) or “Text 2 rejects the use of technology” (not what it says).</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 3 · The traps", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<ul>\n<li><b>The half-true choice.</b> It gets Text 2 exactly right but misstates Text 1, or the reverse. Check both halves. If a choice says “Text 2 questions the rigor of the study” and Text 2 called the study well supported, the choice invented a challenge.</li>\n<li><b>The extreme.</b> “Completely invalidates”, “wholly rejects”, “entirely explains”. Academic texts rarely demolish each other; they complicate, limit or refine. If a choice uses total language, check whether the text goes that far.</li>\n<li><b>Inventing beliefs.</b> “Text 2's author would reject all use of drones in agriculture.” The text only mentioned targeted methods. Never extend an author's view beyond the text. Class 13 (Inferences) builds on this.</li>\n<li><b>Reading “not surprising” as dismissive.</b> If Text 2 calls the finding “unsurprising” and the study “valuable”, both halves matter: the result seems like common sense, and the study gives it solid support. That is a qualified endorsement.</li>\n</ul>"},
      {"title": "Part 4 · Worked example", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<blockquote><b>Text 1.</b> A study found that students who handwrote notes retained more than students who typed them, suggesting handwriting's slowness forces deeper processing.<br><br>\n<b>Text 2.</b> The retention advantage is well documented, but it likely reflects that handwritten notes are shorter and more selective, not that handwriting itself deepens thinking.<br><br>\n<b>Question.</b> How would the author of Text 2 respond to the interpretation offered in Text 1?</blockquote>\n<p><b>Notes.</b> Text 1 = “handwriting causes deeper processing.” Text 2 = “the advantage is real, but the cause is different.” Relationship: accepts the result, disputes the explanation.</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Choice: Text 2's author would…</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>A) dispute that handwriting offers any advantage</td><td>Too extreme. Text 2 says the advantage is well documented.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>B) propose an alternative explanation for the advantage</b></td><td><b>Correct.</b> Real advantage, different cause.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>C) question whether the study measured retention</td><td>Invented. Text 2 never doubts the measurement.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D) endorse handwriting as superior for all note-taking contexts</td><td>Invented extension.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The answer is B. The relationship label did the work, without reading any choice twice.</p>"}
    ]
  },
  "U5": {
    lead: "<p>These questions share one object: a line. You will see four shapes: equations with two variables (12<i>x</i> + 28<i>y</i> = 2016), slope questions, “which point lies on the line?”, and questions about what a coefficient means in a word problem. The skills transfer between all of them.</p>",
    parts: [
      {"title": "Part 1 · The core idea", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<p>A linear equation in two variables does not have one solution. It has infinitely many: every point (<i>x</i>, <i>y</i>) that satisfies it is a solution, and plotting them all gives a straight line. <b>A line is a picture of an equation, and a point is a solution when it sits on the line.</b></p>\n<p>Two forms matter. <b>Slope-intercept form</b> is <i>y</i> = <i>mx</i> + <i>b</i>, where <i>m</i> is the slope and <i>b</i> is the <i>y</i>-intercept. <b>Standard form</b> is <i>Ax</i> + <i>By</i> = <i>C</i>. You can always convert between them, but you do not always need to, and knowing when is half the skill.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 2 · Slope", "mins": "about 6 min", "html": "<p><b>Slope is the rate of change:</b> how much <i>y</i> changes for each unit change in <i>x</i>.</p>\n<blockquote><div class=\"lw-work\"><div>m = (y₂ − y₁) / (x₂ − x₁)</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>A slope of 3 means every time <i>x</i> goes up 1, <i>y</i> goes up 3. A slope of −2 means every time <i>x</i> goes up 1, <i>y</i> goes down 2. Slope is “rise over run”, and it is the number the SAT asks about most.</p>\n<p>In word problems, slope is the <i>per-unit</i> meaning: dollars per ticket, metres per second, pages per day. If a question asks what the slope represents, answer with the units, not the number.</p>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-mistake\"><b>Common mistake.</b> In standard form <i>Ax</i> + <i>By</i> = <i>C</i>, the slope is −<i>A</i>/<i>B</i>, not <i>A</i>. For 12<i>x</i> + 28<i>y</i> = 2016 the slope is −12/28 = −3/7. The sign comes from rearranging: 28<i>y</i> = −12<i>x</i> + 2016. Students who see 12 and take it as the slope miss these questions every time.</p>\n<p><b>Parallel and perpendicular lines</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Parallel lines have the <b>same slope</b>.</li>\n<li>Perpendicular lines have slopes that multiply to −1: <b>flip the fraction and change the sign</b>. The negative reciprocal of 1/3 is −3; of −3/7 it is 7/3.</li>\n<li>The exception: a vertical line (<i>x</i> = 4, undefined slope) is perpendicular to a horizontal line (<i>y</i> = −2, slope 0). Do not use the reciprocal rule on undefined slopes.</li>\n</ul>"},
      {"title": "Part 3 · Points on a line", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<p>“Which point lies on the line?” has one test: <b>plug it in</b>. A point (<i>a</i>, <i>b</i>) is on the line exactly when substituting <i>a</i> for <i>x</i> and <i>b</i> for <i>y</i> makes the equation true. No graphing, no form conversion.</p>\n<blockquote>Is (20, 60) on 12<i>x</i> + 28<i>y</i> = 2016?\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>12(20) + 28(60) = 240 + 1680 = 1920 ≠ 2016 → not on the line</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>The same test answers harder-sounding questions.</p>\n<blockquote>The line 3<i>x</i> + <i>ky</i> = 15 passes through (2, 3). What is <i>k</i>?\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>3(2) + k(3) = 15 → 6 + 3k = 15 → k = 3</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>“A point on the line” is always a substitution in disguise.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 4 · What the equation means in context", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<p>In word problems each piece of <i>Ax</i> + <i>By</i> = <i>C</i> has a meaning. Take 12<i>x</i> + 28<i>y</i> = 2016, where <i>x</i> is standard parcels and <i>y</i> is heavy parcels:</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Piece</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>12</td><td>dollars per standard parcel</td></tr>\n<tr><td>28</td><td>dollars per heavy parcel</td></tr>\n<tr><td>12<i>x</i> and 28<i>y</i></td><td>the totals from each type</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2016</td><td>the combined total</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>“How much more does a heavy parcel cost than a standard one?” The answer is 28 − 12 = 16, the <i>difference of the coefficients</i>, not 28<i>y</i> − 12<i>x</i>. The counts <i>x</i> and <i>y</i> do not matter for a per-item comparison.</p>\n<p>A question that asks you to interpret 10<i>x</i> in a lumber problem wants “the total boards used for the bookshelves”, not “boards per bookshelf”, which is just 10. Reading the term's role correctly is the whole question.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 5 · The Desmos workflow", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<p>Desmos graphs standard form directly: type <code>12x+28y=2016</code> and the line appears. Two high-value uses:</p>\n<p><b>Building a line from two points.</b> When a question gives two points and asks for a <i>y</i>-value elsewhere on the line, enter the points in a table (columns <i>x</i>₁ and <i>y</i>₁), then type <code>y_1~mx_1+b</code>. Desmos fits the exact line. Define <code>f(x)=mx+b</code> and evaluate <code>f(target x)</code>. This also solves “find the missing coordinate” questions.</p>\n<p><b>Solving for unknown coefficients.</b> If the line <i>bx</i> + <i>cy</i> = 10 passes through two known points, put the points in a table and enter <code>b x_1 + c y_1 ~ 10</code>. Desmos fits both constants at once. Then substitute the values back into both points to verify, because regression gives decimals that you should convert to exact fractions.</p>\n<p>For parallel and perpendicular questions, reasoning by hand is faster than graphing: compute −<i>A</i>/<i>B</i> for each line and compare. The graph is a visual confirmation, not the decision-maker.</p>"}
    ]
  },
  "U6": {
    lead: "<p>A system is two or more equations that must be true at the same time. On the SAT you will see systems like 3<i>x</i> + 2<i>y</i> = 12 and <i>y</i> = 2<i>x</i> − 1, and be asked for a solution, one variable, or how many solutions exist. This is also the class where the Desmos intersection method becomes your biggest time-saver in Math.</p>",
    parts: [
      {"title": "Part 1 · The core idea", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<p>One linear equation in two variables has infinitely many solutions, a whole line of them. A system of two asks which points satisfy <b>both</b> lines at once. Geometrically: where do the two lines cross?</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Picture</th><th>What it means</th><th>Solutions</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>One intersection</td><td>The lines cross once</td><td>One (<i>x</i>, <i>y</i>) pair</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Parallel lines</td><td>Same slope, different intercepts</td><td>None</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Same line</td><td>One equation is a disguised copy of the other</td><td>Infinitely many</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>This mirrors Class 3. There, one equation simplified to a false statement (no solution) or a true one (infinitely many). With systems the same two special cases return as parallel and identical lines.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 2 · The three algebraic methods", "mins": "about 6 min", "html": "<p>Know all three, because different questions reward different ones.</p>\n<p><b>Graphing.</b> Plot both lines and read the intersection. Good for understanding, too slow by hand for the test.</p>\n<p><b>Substitution.</b> Use it when one equation already says <i>y</i> = … or <i>x</i> = …</p>\n<blockquote><i>y</i> = 2<i>x</i> − 1 and 3<i>x</i> + 2<i>y</i> = 12\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>3x + 2(2x − 1) = 12 → 3x + 4x − 2 = 12 → 7x = 14 → x = 2</div><div>y = 2(2) − 1 = 3 → solution (2, 3)</div></div></blockquote>\n<p><b>Elimination.</b> Use it when both equations are in <i>Ax</i> + <i>By</i> = <i>C</i> form. Line them up and add or subtract to remove one variable.</p>\n<blockquote>3<i>x</i> + 2<i>y</i> = 12 and <i>x</i> − 2<i>y</i> = −4\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Add: +2y and −2y cancel → 4x = 8 → x = 2 → plug back in → y = 3</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>Sometimes you must multiply one equation first so the coefficients match.</p>\n<blockquote>2<i>x</i> + 3<i>y</i> = 7 and 5<i>x</i> − <i>y</i> = 9\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Multiply the second by 3 → 15x − 3y = 27</div><div>Add → 17x = 34 → x = 2 → y = 1</div></div></blockquote>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-info\"><b>Verify.</b> Plug the solution into <i>both</i> original equations. If it fails one, you made an arithmetic slip.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 3 · The special-answer questions", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<p>“How many solutions does the system have?” and its harder form: “The system has no solution (or infinitely many solutions); what is the value of the constant?”</p>\n<p>The rule: lines are parallel or identical when the <i>x</i>- and <i>y</i>-coefficients of one equation are the same multiple of the other's. Then the constants decide which case it is.</p>\n<blockquote><b>Example.</b> 2<i>x</i> + 3<i>y</i> = 6 and 4<i>x</i> + 6<i>y</i> = <i>c</i>\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Divide the second by 2 → 2x + 3y = c/2</div><div>c = 12 → identical equations → infinitely many solutions</div><div>c ≠ 12 → parallel lines → no solution</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>No value of <i>c</i> gives exactly one solution, because these lines can only be identical or parallel, never crossing. The SAT asks for this reasoning with uglier numbers.</p>\n<p><b>Quick procedure.</b> Divide the second equation by whatever makes its <i>x</i>-coefficient match the first's, then compare the remaining pieces.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 4 · The Desmos method", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<p>This is the highest-value shortcut in the Math section. Type each equation on its own line in Desmos. Two lines appear; click the intersection point. The coordinates are the solution.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The question asks for <i>x</i>? Read the <i>x</i>-coordinate. For <i>y</i>, read the <i>y</i>-coordinate.</li>\n<li>It asks for something built from the solution, like 2<i>x</i> + 3<i>y</i>? Compute it from the coordinates.</li>\n<li><b>No solution</b> shows as two parallel lines. <b>Infinitely many</b> shows as one visible line drawn twice.</li>\n<li>For a system with an unknown constant, like <i>ax</i> + by = 12, graph what you know and use the given condition (a point the line passes through) to find the unknown.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>When to still use algebra.</b> When the question wants one variable and the setup is simple, elimination can be faster than typing two equations.</p>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-tip\"><b>Tip.</b> For “which ordered pair is the solution” questions with small numbers, plug in the choices. Test each pair in both equations; only one satisfies both. It is often 30 seconds in total and immune to sign errors.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 5 · Systems in word problems", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<p>A word problem becomes a system when two quantities have two relationships.</p>\n<blockquote>A cinema sold 220 tickets. Adult tickets cost $12 and child tickets cost $7, and total sales were $2100.\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Count: a + c = 220</div><div>Money: 12a + 7c = 2100</div><div>Solution: a = 112, c = 108</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>The signal that you need a system: <b>two unknowns and two independent facts about them.</b> Count plus total cost is the classic pattern. It also appears as perimeter plus area, sum plus product, and speed-time-distance pairs.</p>\n<p>Define the variables in words before writing equations. Write one equation per fact, in the order the facts appear. Then eliminate or use Desmos.</p>"}
    ]
  },
  "U7": {
    lead: "<p>This type covers two related questions: “What is the main idea of the text?” and “According to the text, which statement is true?” They are the most direct reading questions on the test. The answer is always in the passage, and the method is mostly about avoiding choices that are almost right.</p>",
    parts: [
      {"title": "Part 1 · The core idea", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<p>A <b>central idea</b> is what the whole text is about and the point it makes about that thing: topic plus point. “Bees” is a topic. “Bees communicate food locations through dance” is an idea, because a claim is attached.</p>\n<p>A <b>detail</b> is a specific piece of information the text states. Detail questions are pure retrieval: the answer is in the passage word for word or close to it.</p>\n<p>The wrong answers are built differently for each. On main idea questions they are <b>mis-sized</b>: too narrow, too broad, or true but not central. On detail questions they are <b>distortions</b>: swapped subjects, reversed conditions, wrong actors, or reasonable things the text never says.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 2 · The main idea method", "mins": "about 5 min", "html": "<ol>\n<li><b>Read the whole text.</b> The first sentence is often not the idea. Short SAT passages frequently save their point for the final sentence, which may qualify or reverse what came before.</li>\n<li><b>Write a one-sentence summary in your own words.</b> Not “the text talks about ants” but “ants solve traffic problems that human engineers struggle with.” If your summary is only a topic, ask: what about the ants? What is the point of telling me this?</li>\n<li><b>Size-check each choice against your summary.</b>\n  <ul>\n  <li><i>Too narrow:</i> one supporting detail raised to the level of the main idea. Does the whole passage serve this, or one sentence?</li>\n  <li><i>Too broad:</i> covers something the passage only touches. Does the passage develop this, or mention it once?</li>\n  <li><i>True but not central:</i> accurate and straight from the passage, but not what the passage is about. This is the most dangerous one. Truth is not the test here; centrality is.</li>\n  </ul></li>\n<li><b>Watch the last sentence.</b> It often carries the real point, especially in science passages where the setup comes first and the significance last. If your summary ignores the final sentence, it is probably incomplete.</li>\n</ol>"},
      {"title": "Part 3 · The detail method", "mins": "about 5 min", "html": "<ol>\n<li><b>Read the question first</b>, then the passage with the question in mind. Detail questions reward targeted reading.</li>\n<li><b>Predict the answer, then find it.</b> If the question asks what the 2018 study concluded, locate the study and read exactly what it concluded. Then match. The danger is a choice that is almost the sentence, with one actor or condition swapped.</li>\n<li><b>Use the anchor-word check on near-misses.</b> When two choices share the key terms, compare them word by word with the passage. The difference is usually one small word: “most” vs. “some”, “caused” vs. “correlated with”, “juveniles” vs. “adults”, “prevented” vs. “reduced”.</li>\n<li><b>Reject the reasonable-but-absent choice.</b> Some wrong choices are true in the real world and never stated in the passage. Your prior knowledge is not evidence. If it is not in the text, it is not an answer.</li>\n</ol>\n<blockquote><b>Example.</b> “The 1990s revival of vinyl records surprised industry analysts, though sales remained well below their 1970s peak, and the revival was driven largely by collectors under thirty.”</blockquote>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Wrong choice</th><th>What it changes</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Vinyl sales in the 1990s exceeded their 1970s levels</td><td>Reverses a fact</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The revival was driven by industry marketing campaigns</td><td>Swaps the actor: the text credits collectors</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Analysts predicted the vinyl revival</td><td>Swaps the verb: they were surprised</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The correct choice must keep all three facts, including “below their 1970s peak”, which weak choices often quietly drop.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 4 · Two different rhythms", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Rhythm</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Main idea</td><td>Read everything first, summarize, then match</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Detail</td><td>Read the question first, hunt, then match word by word</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Mixing the rhythms causes careless errors. Skimming on a detail question means you never found the sentence. Word-matching on a main idea question means you picked a fragment as the main idea.</p>\n<p>When a choice set has both a summary and a true statement, the main idea question wants the one that covers the whole text. If you are torn, ask: if I crossed this out, what would the passage lose? The main idea is the thing the passage cannot lose.</p>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-tip\"><b>Tip.</b> On detail questions, put your finger on the sentence that answers the question before you look at the choices. If you cannot point to it, you are answering from memory, and memory is where the trap choices live.</p>"}
    ]
  },
  "U8": {
    lead: "<p>These questions give you a claim and ask which choice proves it. On the Digital SAT each one is a single question with its own short text, in two main forms: “Which quotation from the text most effectively illustrates the claim?” and “Which finding, if true, would most directly support (or weaken) the hypothesis?”</p>",
    parts: [
      {"title": "Part 1 · The core idea", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<p>The claim is given to you. Your job is not to interpret the passage but to find the choice that <b>demonstrates</b> that exact claim. Every choice is on-topic. Only one proves the claim.</p>\n<p>A useful shortcut: for each choice, ask “what claim would this prove?” If the answer is not the claim in the question, the choice is out, however relevant it sounds.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 2 · The method", "mins": "about 6 min", "html": "<ol>\n<li><b>Restate the claim in simple words.</b> Know exactly what needs proving before you touch the choices. “Good writers write for general readers” needs evidence about both the writers and that audience. A quote describing only readers' preferences does not show what the writers do.</li>\n<li><b>Run two checks on each choice.</b>\n  <ul>\n  <li><i>Does it do the job?</i> Does it demonstrate the claim, or only relate to the topic?</li>\n  <li><i>Is it accurate?</i> Does it match the passage, with the right subject, action and scope?</li>\n  </ul></li>\n<li><b>Know the three ways a choice fails.</b>\n  <ul>\n  <li><i>Wrong support:</i> it proves a different claim.</li>\n  <li><i>Too weak:</i> it gestures at the idea without showing it. “She enjoyed experimenting” does not prove her early work was innovative; openness to new ideas is a different claim.</li>\n  <li><i>Wrong scope:</i> it proves more or less than asked. If the claim is about one character's unused talent, a quote about a gloomy landscape sets a mood but does not show it.</li>\n  </ul></li>\n<li><b>For support or weaken questions, check the direction.</b> A finding supports a hypothesis when it is what you would expect to see if the hypothesis were true. It weakens the hypothesis when it is the opposite. One wrong choice is usually the right finding for the opposite direction.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-tip\"><b>Tip.</b> When two choices both look supportive, the tiebreak is precision. Pick the one that states the claim directly and completely, including the mechanism or reason, not just the conclusion.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 3 · Common patterns", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<p><b>Matching attitude, not mood.</b> A passage can describe a gloomy setting while the claim is about a character's choices. Do not pick a quote because it shares the mood. Find the quote where the character's action shows the claim.</p>\n<p><b>Poetry questions.</b> In a poem like “To Autumn”, lines that sound as if they describe a person may address the season, because the poem treats autumn as a person. Use the setup sentence to identify who or what is being addressed before choosing evidence.</p>\n<p><b>The outside-idea variant.</b> Some questions give an idea from outside the text and ask which quote best matches it. Translate the idea into your own words, then find the sentence that says the same thing. It is a matching task, not interpretation.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 4 · Worked example", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<blockquote>Critics initially dismissed the poet's work. Decades later, scholars reevaluated it and concluded that her unconventional line breaks were deliberate innovations. One scholar argued the line breaks were not merely stylistic quirks; they were her method for controlling pacing and emphasis.<br><br>\n<b>Claim to support.</b> The poet's line breaks were purposeful rather than accidental.</blockquote>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Quote</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>A) “Critics initially dismissed the poet's work.”</td><td>On-topic, but proves dismissal, not purpose.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>B) “…they were her method for controlling pacing and emphasis.”</b></td><td><b>Correct.</b> States the purpose and includes the mechanism.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>C) “…her unconventional line breaks were deliberate innovations.”</td><td>Supportive but weaker. “Deliberate” shows intent without showing what the breaks accomplished.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>B and C both survive the two checks, and precision picks B. Restate the claim, run the checks, apply the tiebreak. No choice needs reading twice.</p>"}
    ]
  },
  "U9": {
    lead: "<p>Quadratics are the heart of the Advanced Math domain. This class covers the foundation: what a quadratic is, its three forms, factoring, multiplying and simplifying expressions, and how a quadratic looks on a graph. Class 11 builds on it with completing the square and the discriminant.</p>",
    parts: [
      {"title": "Part 1 · The core idea", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<p>A quadratic is any expression with <i>x</i>² as its highest power: <i>ax</i>² + <i>bx</i> + <i>c</i>, where <i>a</i> is not zero. Every quadratic graphs as a <b>parabola</b>, a smooth U-shaped curve. If <i>a</i> &gt; 0 it opens upward and has a minimum. If <i>a</i> &lt; 0 it opens downward and has a maximum.</p>\n<p>The three most important points on a parabola:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Roots</b> (zeros, <i>x</i>-intercepts): where the parabola crosses the <i>x</i>-axis, where <i>y</i> = 0. These are the solutions of <i>ax</i>² + <i>bx</i> + <i>c</i> = 0.</li>\n<li><b>The vertex:</b> the turning point, a minimum if it opens up and a maximum if it opens down.</li>\n<li><b>The <i>y</i>-intercept:</b> where it crosses the <i>y</i>-axis, always at (0, <i>c</i>) in standard form.</li>\n</ul>"},
      {"title": "Part 2 · The three forms", "mins": "about 6 min", "html": "<p><b>Each form of a quadratic reveals different information at a glance.</b></p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Looks like</th><th>Shows at once</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Standard</td><td><i>ax</i>² + <i>bx</i> + <i>c</i></td><td>The <i>y</i>-intercept, <i>c</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Factored</td><td><i>a</i>(<i>x</i> − <i>r</i>₁)(<i>x</i> − <i>r</i>₂)</td><td>The roots, <i>r</i>₁ and <i>r</i>₂</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Vertex</td><td><i>a</i>(<i>x</i> − <i>h</i>)² + <i>k</i></td><td>The vertex, (<i>h</i>, <i>k</i>)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Factored form</b> works by the <b>zero product property</b>: the product is zero when either factor is zero. <i>y</i> = (<i>x</i> − 3)(<i>x</i> + 5) has roots <i>x</i> = 3 and <i>x</i> = −5. Watch the signs: (<i>x</i> + 5) gives the root −5, not +5.</p>\n<p><b>Vertex form</b> has the same sign trap. <i>y</i> = (<i>x</i> − 2)² + 7 has vertex (2, 7). <i>y</i> = 3(<i>x</i> + 4)² − 1 has vertex (−4, −1), because <i>x</i> + 4 = <i>x</i> − (−4).</p>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-tip\"><b>Tip.</b> Read the question first to know which form to reach for. Roots asked → factored form. Vertex, minimum or maximum asked → vertex form. <i>y</i>-intercept asked → the <i>c</i> in standard form. If the given form does not show what is asked, the question wants you to convert or factor.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 3 · Factoring", "mins": "about 5 min", "html": "<p>Factoring reverses multiplication: start from <i>x</i>² + <i>bx</i> + <i>c</i> and find (<i>x</i> + <i>p</i>)(<i>x</i> + <i>q</i>). The rule: find two numbers that <b>multiply to <i>c</i> and add to <i>b</i></b>.</p>\n<blockquote><i>x</i>² + 7<i>x</i> + 12\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Multiply to 12, add to 7 → 3 and 4 → (x + 3)(x + 4) → roots −3 and −4</div></div></blockquote>\n<blockquote><i>x</i>² − <i>x</i> − 12\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Multiply to −12, add to −1 → −4 and +3 → (x − 4)(x + 3) → roots 4 and −3</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>Not every quadratic factors nicely. The quadratic formula always works:</p>\n<blockquote><div class=\"lw-work\"><div>x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) / 2a</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>Memorize it, since it is not on the reference sheet. On the test, when the numbers get ugly, Desmos is usually faster.</p>\n<p><b>Difference of squares.</b> <i>x</i>² − 25 = (<i>x</i> + 5)(<i>x</i> − 5). When you see two perfect squares separated by subtraction, factor at once. The SAT uses this constantly, often in disguise: 4<i>x</i>² − 49 = (2<i>x</i> + 7)(2<i>x</i> − 7).</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 4 · Polynomial operations and rational expressions", "mins": "about 5 min", "html": "<p>These are the “which expression is equivalent to…?” questions. They use factoring in reverse and forwards.</p>\n<p><b>Multiplying polynomials.</b> Use the distributive property: every term in the first bracket multiplies every term in the second.</p>\n<blockquote>(2<i>x</i> + 3)(<i>x</i> − 5)\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>2x² − 10x + 3x − 15 = 2x² − 7x − 15</div></div></blockquote>\n<p><b>Adding algebraic fractions.</b> You must find a common denominator first. In general, <i>A</i>/<i>x</i> + <i>B</i>/<i>y</i> = (<i>Ay</i> + <i>Bx</i>) / <i>xy</i>.</p>\n<blockquote>2/<i>x</i> + 3/(<i>x</i> + 1)\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Common denominator: x(x + 1)</div><div>(2(x + 1) + 3x) / (x(x + 1)) = (5x + 2) / (x(x + 1))</div></div></blockquote>\n<p><b>Simplifying.</b> Factor the numerator and the denominator first, then cancel common factors.</p>\n<blockquote>(<i>x</i>² − 9) / (<i>x</i> + 3)\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>(x + 3)(x − 3) / (x + 3) = x − 3</div></div></blockquote>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-mistake\"><b>Common mistake.</b> Cancelling terms that are added, not multiplied. In (<i>x</i>² − 9) / (<i>x</i> + 3) you cannot cancel the <i>x</i>'s or divide 9 by 3. Only whole factors cancel, which is why you factor first.</p>\n<p><b>Rational equations.</b> If <i>P</i>(<i>x</i>) / <i>Q</i>(<i>x</i>) = 0, then <i>P</i>(<i>x</i>) = 0. Always check that your solution does not make the denominator <i>Q</i>(<i>x</i>) zero. Class 21 works through these in full.</p>\n<p><b>Desmos check.</b> For an equivalence question, graph the original expression and each choice. The equivalent one draws exactly the same curve.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 5 · Reading a quadratic graph or table", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<ul>\n<li><b>Roots visible on the <i>x</i>-axis?</b> They are the numbers in factored form. Roots 2 and 5 mean <i>y</i> = <i>a</i>(<i>x</i> − 2)(<i>x</i> − 5) for some <i>a</i>.</li>\n<li><b>Vertex visible?</b> Use vertex form: <i>y</i> = <i>a</i>(<i>x</i> − <i>h</i>)² + <i>k</i>.</li>\n<li><b>Axis of symmetry:</b> the vertical line through the vertex. <b>The vertex sits exactly midway between two points of equal height.</b> If <i>f</i>(2) = <i>f</i>(10), the axis is <i>x</i> = 6. If you also know <i>b</i>, you can find <i>a</i> from <i>x</i> = −<i>b</i>/2<i>a</i>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The midpoint rule works with roots too. If a quadratic has roots 3 and −9, the vertex's <i>x</i>-coordinate is (3 + (−9)) / 2 = −3.</p>\n<p><b>Tables.</b> Each point in a table is one equation, so each point lets you find one unknown coefficient. Three points determine the whole quadratic. Desmos regression handles these cleanly.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 6 · The Desmos workflow", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<ul>\n<li><b>Find roots:</b> type the equation and click where the curve crosses the <i>x</i>-axis. This replaces factoring and the quadratic formula for “what are the solutions” questions.</li>\n<li><b>Find the vertex, maximum or minimum:</b> type the function and click the turning point, which Desmos marks. Read the <i>x</i>-coordinate for “at what value of <i>x</i>” and the <i>y</i>-coordinate for the maximum or minimum value.</li>\n<li><b>Solve <i>ax</i>² + <i>bx</i> + <i>c</i> = <i>d</i>:</b> type the quadratic and the horizontal line <i>y</i> = <i>d</i>. The <i>x</i>-coordinates of the intersections are the solutions.</li>\n<li><b>Unknown coefficient with a given point or root:</b> define the function with a slider for the unknown, plot the given point and adjust. Then verify by substituting the value exactly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When the question is conceptual (“which form shows the maximum”, “which is equivalent”), reasoning by hand is faster. Use Desmos when numbers or coordinates are asked for.</p>"}
    ]
  },
  "U10": {
    lead: "<p>Same skill as Class 8, new evidence type. You get a short text with one table or graph, and the question asks “Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the claim?” (or to complete the text). The four choices are statements about the data. This is where the data-heavy science passages live, and the method is mechanical once you learn it.</p>",
    parts: [
      {"title": "Part 1 · The core idea", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<p>Take a claim like “plant species X grew taller under blue light than under red light.” The right choice has to contain exactly two things: the <b>variables</b> the claim mentions (species X, blue light, red light, height) and the <b>comparison</b> the claim makes (taller, so bigger height numbers). A choice about other variables, or about the right variables with numbers pointing the wrong way, does not support the claim.</p>\n<p>Two checks, in order:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Does it do the job?</b> Right variables, right comparison.</li>\n<li><b>Is it true in the table?</b> Do the actual numbers say what the choice says?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wrong choices fail one check each. Some are accurate but irrelevant. Others are relevant but misread the data.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 2 · The method", "mins": "about 6 min", "html": "<ol>\n<li><b>Translate the claim into a data requirement.</b> Before looking at the table, restate what the data must show: “this claim needs more sightings of birds tagged in 2005 than of birds tagged in 1995, in later years.” Think of it as a checklist of variables plus a direction.</li>\n<li><b>Filter first, then compare.</b> This is the most valuable habit in the topic. If the question asks about a specific group, restrict your attention to that group before reading any numbers. For “the earliest visual imaging source”, first cross out every row that is not visual imaging, then find the earliest date among what is left. The earliest date anywhere in the table answers a different question, and the trap choice is built from that mistake.</li>\n<li><b>Do the arithmetic.</b> Write the comparison out with the actual numbers. If the claim is about which difference is larger, subtract both: one pair differs by 20, the other by 4. Do not eyeball it.</li>\n<li><b>Read what the measurement means.</b> In the classic antibiotics question, a bigger clear zone in a petri dish means more inhibition of bacteria, so the bacteria were <i>less</i> resistant. Students see “bigger number” and conclude “more resistance”, and the whole question flips. Ask: what does a larger value mean in this experiment?</li>\n</ol>"},
      {"title": "Part 3 · The traps", "mins": "about 5 min", "html": "<ul>\n<li><b>Right numbers, wrong group.</b> The choice cites 2010 data and the claim needs 2015, or it cites juveniles and the claim is about adults. Accurate and useless.</li>\n<li><b>Reversed meaning.</b> Bigger is not always more of what the claim says. A smaller time is faster; a smaller error is more accurate.</li>\n<li><b>True but irrelevant.</b> The same trap as in textual evidence: on-topic is not the same as supportive.</li>\n<li><b>Level vs. change.</b> If the claim says “populations increased”, you need two time points, not one snapshot.</li>\n<li><b>Correlation dressed as causation.</b> Two variables rising together supports “they are associated”, not “one causes the other”. Class 17 covers what a sample can and cannot show.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-mistake\"><b>Common mistake.</b> Skipping the arithmetic because the table “looks like” it shows the trend. Under time pressure, students match a rising column to the word “increase” and stop. Write the two numbers down and subtract. It costs five seconds.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 4 · Worked example", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<blockquote><b>Claim.</b> The results support the hypothesis that the new filter removes more particulates than the standard filter.</blockquote>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Filter</th><th>Clarity score after one pass</th><th>Filters tested</th><th>Cost per filter</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>New</td><td>87</td><td>5</td><td>$14</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Standard</td><td>62</td><td>5</td><td>$8</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Translate the claim.</b> It needs a removal-related measurement for both filters, with new greater than standard.</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Choice</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>A) Water passed through the new filter scored 87 for clarity; the standard filter scored 62.</b></td><td><b>Correct.</b> Right measurement, and 87 &gt; 62 in the right direction.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>B) The new filter cost $14, compared with $8 for the standard filter.</td><td>True but irrelevant. Cost is not performance.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>C) Five filters of each type were tested.</td><td>True but irrelevant. Sample size is not performance.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D) The standard filter scored 87 for clarity, higher than the new filter.</td><td>Right variable, but not true in the table.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>If the table had shown new = 55 and standard = 70, the clarity row would <i>weaken</i> the claim. Direction matters as much as variables.</p>\n<p>Some choices state a computed figure (“the difference was approximately 25 points”). Apply the same checks: right group, right numbers, right direction, arithmetic done. Here 87 − 62 = 25.</p>"}
    ]
  },
  "U11": {
    lead: "<p>Part 1 gave you the three forms and factoring. This class adds the discriminant, completing the square, and the questions that combine them, including the “how many solutions” and “for what value of the constant” problems that fill the hard half of Module 2.</p>",
    parts: [
      {"title": "Part 1 · The discriminant", "mins": "about 5 min", "html": "<p>For <i>ax</i>² + <i>bx</i> + <i>c</i> = 0, the expression <i>b</i>² − 4<i>ac</i> is the <b>discriminant</b>. It tells you how many real solutions there are without solving anything.</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th><i>b</i>² − 4<i>ac</i></th><th>Real solutions</th><th>Picture</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Positive</td><td>Two</td><td>The parabola crosses the <i>x</i>-axis twice</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Zero</td><td>Exactly one</td><td>The vertex sits on the <i>x</i>-axis</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Negative</td><td>None</td><td>The parabola never reaches the <i>x</i>-axis</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The discriminant counts crossings. The vertex-on-the-axis case is the boundary between crossing twice and not at all.</p>\n<blockquote><b>Classic question.</b> <i>x</i>² + <i>kx</i> + 9 = 0 has exactly one real solution. What is a possible value of <i>k</i>?\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Set the discriminant to zero: k² − 4(1)(9) = 0 → k² = 36 → k = 6 or k = −6</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>The test often asks for <i>one possible value</i>. The negative <i>k</i> is the answer students forget, because they stop at the first square root.</p>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-mistake\"><b>Common mistake.</b> Sign slips in <i>b</i>² − 4<i>ac</i>. Write it out in full before computing. For 2<i>x</i>² − 5<i>x</i> + 3 = 0: (−5)² − 4(2)(3) = 25 − 24 = 1. The square removes the sign of <i>b</i>. When <i>c</i> is negative, the last term turns positive: for 2<i>x</i>² − 5<i>x</i> − 3 = 0 it is 25 − 4(2)(−3) = 25 + 24 = 49.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 2 · Completing the square", "mins": "about 6 min", "html": "<p>Completing the square rewrites <i>x</i>² + <i>bx</i> + constant as a squared term plus a leftover. It is the tool for reaching vertex form and for “what is the minimum value” questions.</p>\n<p>The method for <i>x</i>² + <i>bx</i>: <b>take half of <i>b</i>, square it, add it and subtract it.</b></p>\n<blockquote><i>x</i>² + 6<i>x</i> + 2\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Half of 6 is 3, squared is 9</div><div>(x² + 6x + 9) − 9 + 2 = (x + 3)² − 7</div><div>Vertex: (−3, −7)</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>The −9 + 2 step is where errors happen. You added 9 inside the brackets, so you must subtract 9 to keep the value the same.</p>\n<p><b>Why it works.</b> (<i>x</i> + <i>h</i>)² expands to <i>x</i>² + 2<i>hx</i> + <i>h</i>². The middle coefficient is twice <i>h</i>, so <i>h</i> = <i>b</i>/2 and the square you need is <i>h</i>². Knowing this, a fraction does not stop you: <i>x</i>² + 3<i>x</i> needs (3/2)² = 9/4.</p>\n<p><b>Vertex and minimum or maximum questions.</b> <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = <i>x</i>² − 8<i>x</i> + 10 = (<i>x</i> − 4)² − 6. Since (<i>x</i> − 4)² is never negative, the smallest value of <i>f</i> is −6, at <i>x</i> = 4. “What is the minimum value of the function” wants <i>k</i>, not the <i>x</i>-coordinate. Read which one is asked.</p>\n<p><b>Equivalent-expression questions.</b> “Which expression is equivalent to <i>x</i>² + 6<i>x</i> + 2?” with choices in completed-square form. Same computation; the test is checking your bookkeeping.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 3 · The vertex and axis of symmetry", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<p>Two formulas to memorize. Neither is on the reference sheet.</p>\n<blockquote><div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Axis of symmetry: x = −b / 2a</div><div>Vertex: ( −b / 2a , f(−b / 2a) )</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>The symmetry rule from Class 9 still earns points: the vertex sits midway between any two points of equal height, and midway between the two roots. If <i>f</i>(1) = <i>f</i>(7), the axis is <i>x</i> = 4. Since the axis is also −<i>b</i>/2<i>a</i>, you get a relation between <i>a</i> and <i>b</i> for free. Hard Module 2 questions chain exactly this: they give <i>f</i>(<i>p</i>) = <i>f</i>(<i>q</i>) and ask for a coefficient.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 4 · Nonlinear systems", "mins": "about 5 min", "html": "<p>A nonlinear system is a quadratic combined with another shape, such as a line or a circle. The goal is usually the <b>number of intersection points</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Algebraic method</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Substitute the line (<i>y</i> = <i>mx</i> + <i>b</i>) into the quadratic (<i>y</i> = <i>ax</i>² + <i>cx</i> + <i>d</i>).</li>\n<li>Move everything to one side so the equation equals 0. You now have a single quadratic.</li>\n<li>Check the <b>discriminant</b> of this new quadratic.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Discriminant</th><th>Intersection points</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Positive</td><td>2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Zero</td><td>1 (the line is tangent to the curve)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Negative</td><td>0</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>The line <i>y</i> = 2<i>x</i> − 1 intersects the parabola <i>y</i> = <i>x</i>² − 4 at how many points?\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Substitute: x² − 4 = 2x − 1 → x² − 2x − 3 = 0</div><div>Discriminant: (−2)² − 4(1)(−3) = 16 → positive → two points</div><div>Factoring confirms it: (x − 3)(x + 1) = 0 → x = 3 or x = −1</div></div></blockquote>\n<blockquote>The line <i>y</i> = 2<i>x</i> + <i>k</i> is tangent to the parabola <i>y</i> = <i>x</i>². What is <i>k</i>?\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Substitute: x² = 2x + k → x² − 2x − k = 0</div><div>Tangent means discriminant = 0: (−2)² − 4(1)(−k) = 4 + 4k = 0 → k = −1</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>“How many intersection points” is “how many real solutions does the combined equation have”. <b>A nonlinear system is a quadratic with a second equation attached.</b> The same idea answers “at how many points does the parabola intersect the <i>x</i>-axis?”, which is the discriminant of the quadratic itself.</p>\n<p><b>Desmos method.</b> Type both equations exactly as they are. The number of times the shapes cross is the number of real solutions. If the question asks for coordinates, click the intersection points to read the (<i>x</i>, <i>y</i>) values.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 5 · The Desmos workflow for hard questions", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<ul>\n<li><b>One real solution:</b> graph <i>y</i> = (left side) and <i>y</i> = (right side) as separate expressions. Exactly one solution shows as the line resting tangent to the parabola. To test a constant, define it as a slider and watch the tangency appear at the right value.</li>\n<li><b>Minimum or maximum value:</b> graph the function and click the vertex. Read both coordinates, then decide which one the question wants.</li>\n<li><b>Unknown coefficient:</b> define the function with the unknown as a slider, add the given condition (a plotted point, a root, a value such as <i>f</i>(3) = 10) and slide until it is met. Then verify by substitution, because slider positions are approximate.</li>\n<li><b>Equivalent expressions:</b> graph the original and each choice. The equivalent one draws the identical curve. Slower than completing the square for simple cases, but reliable when the expansion gets messy.</li>\n</ul>"}
    ]
  },
  "U12": {
    lead: "<p>This class goes deeper into Advanced Math: exponential functions, growth and decay models, and radicals. They appear constantly in Module 2, especially as word problems about populations, money and decay. One mental move unlocks nearly all of them: read the base as a <i>multiplier per time step</i>.</p>",
    parts: [
      {"title": "Part 1 · The core idea", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<p>An exponential function looks like <i>f</i>(<i>t</i>) = <i>a</i> · <i>b</i><sup><i>t</i></sup>. The variable is in the <b>exponent</b>. Every time <i>t</i> increases by 1, the output is multiplied by <i>b</i>.</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Behaviour</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><i>f</i>(<i>t</i>) = 500 · 2<sup><i>t</i></sup></td><td>Doubles every step</td></tr>\n<tr><td><i>f</i>(<i>t</i>) = 100 · (0.5)<sup><i>t</i></sup></td><td>Halves every step (decay)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><i>f</i>(<i>t</i>) = 80 · (1.05)<sup><i>t</i></sup></td><td>Grows 5% per step</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><i>a</i> is the <b>starting value</b>: at <i>t</i> = 0, <i>b</i>⁰ = 1, so <i>f</i>(0) = <i>a</i>. <i>b</i> is the <b>multiplier per step</b>.</p>\n<p>Compare with linear. Linear <i>adds</i> the same amount each step (+3 each time). Exponential <i>multiplies</i> by the same amount each step (×1.05 each time). That contrast is itself a tested question: “which situation is modeled by an exponential function?”</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 2 · Reading percent change into the base", "mins": "about 6 min", "html": "<p>This is the skill the SAT tests most. Every percent change becomes a multiplier.</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Change per period</th><th>Base</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Increase of 5%</td><td>1.05</td><td>100% + 5% = 105% of the old amount</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Decrease of 12%</td><td>0.88</td><td>You keep 88%</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Doubling / halving / tripling</td><td>2 / 0.5 / 3</td><td></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-mistake\"><b>Common mistake.</b> “Decreases by 40%” is base 0.60, not 0.40: you keep 60%. And “increases by 700%” does not mean multiply by 7. The final amount is 800% of the original, a factor of 8. An increase of <i>p</i>% is always 1 + <i>p</i>/100.</p>\n<p><b>Time steps that are not 1.</b> If a quantity grows 2% per quarter and <i>t</i> is in years, there are 4 steps per year: <i>a</i>(1.02)<sup>4<i>t</i></sup>. The exponent must count how many <i>steps</i> have passed. If the growth is <i>p</i>% every <i>d</i> days and <i>t</i> is in days, write <i>a</i>(1 + <i>p</i>/100)<sup><i>t</i>/<i>d</i></sup>. The check: at <i>t</i> = <i>d</i> the exponent is exactly 1, one full step.</p>\n<p><b>Finding the rate from a model.</b> In <i>f</i>(<i>t</i>) = 340(1.4)<sup><i>t</i>/3</sup>, the exponent divides by 3, so the base applies once every 3 units of time: the quantity grows 40% every three days. Read a fraction in the exponent as “one step per <i>d</i> units”. For <i>p</i>(<i>t</i>) = 650 · 3<sup><i>t</i>/240</sup>, the population triples every 240 days.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 3 · Growth and decay word problems", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<ol>\n<li>Identify the starting value (<i>a</i>).</li>\n<li>Identify the percent change per step and convert it to a multiplier. Watch the increase-by-700% trap.</li>\n<li>Identify the step length and set the exponent so that one step makes it equal 1.</li>\n<li>Plug in what is asked.</li>\n</ol>\n<blockquote>A culture of 340 bacteria triples every 5 hours. Which function models the population after <i>t</i> hours?\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Start 340, multiplier 3, step length 5 → f(t) = 340 · 3^(t/5)</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>The wrong choices test the failure points: 3<i>t</i> as a product where the exponent should be, 5/<i>t</i> where <i>t</i>/5 should be, or base 2 (confusing tripling with doubling).</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 4 · Radicals", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<p><b>Fraction exponents are roots:</b> <i>x</i><sup>1/2</sup> = √<i>x</i>, <i>x</i><sup>1/3</sup> = ∛<i>x</i>, and in general <i>x</i><sup><i>m</i>/<i>n</i></sup> is the <i>n</i>th root of <i>x</i>, raised to the power <i>m</i>.</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><i>x</i><sup><i>a</i></sup> · <i>x</i><sup><i>b</i></sup> = <i>x</i><sup><i>a</i>+<i>b</i></sup></td><td>Same base, multiplying: add exponents</td></tr>\n<tr><td><i>x</i><sup><i>a</i></sup> / <i>x</i><sup><i>b</i></sup> = <i>x</i><sup><i>a</i>−<i>b</i></sup></td><td>Same base, dividing: subtract exponents</td></tr>\n<tr><td>(<i>x</i><sup><i>a</i></sup>)<sup><i>b</i></sup> = <i>x</i><sup><i>ab</i></sup></td><td>Power of a power: multiply exponents</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Solving radical equations:</b> isolate the radical, square both sides, solve, then check.</p>\n<blockquote>√<i>x</i> = <i>x</i> − 2\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Square: x = x² − 4x + 4 → x² − 5x + 4 = 0 → (x − 4)(x − 1) = 0</div><div>Check x = 4: √4 = 2 and 4 − 2 = 2 → works</div><div>Check x = 1: √1 = 1 but 1 − 2 = −1 → extraneous</div><div>Answer: x = 4</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>Squaring can create fake solutions. Checking in the original equation is not optional.</p>\n<p><b>A radical in a denominator.</b> If an equation contains <i>k</i>/√<i>x</i>, then √<i>x</i> cannot be zero, so <i>x</i> &gt; 0. Domain checks like this appear on hard questions: watch for choices that break the domain.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 5 · Desmos applications", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<ul>\n<li><b>“After how many years does the population reach 5000?”</b> Type the model and the line <i>y</i> = 5000. The <i>x</i>-coordinate of the intersection is the answer.</li>\n<li><b>Comparing growth:</b> type both models. The straight one is linear; the one that curves away is exponential.</li>\n<li><b>Radical equations:</b> graph <i>y</i> = √<i>x</i> and <i>y</i> = <i>x</i> − 2. The graph shows only the real intersection, <i>x</i> = 4. The extraneous <i>x</i> = 1 never appears, which makes Desmos a quick check on your algebra.</li>\n<li><b>Fraction exponents:</b> Desmos evaluates 8<sup>2/3</sup> directly. For equivalent-expression questions, plug the same number into the original and each choice and compare the values.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-tip\"><b>Tip.</b> Logarithms never appear on the Digital SAT. When a question seems to need one (“after how long will the value reach…?”), it is a Desmos intersection question: graph the model and the target value, and read the <i>x</i>-coordinate.</p>"}
    ]
  },
  "U13": {
    lead: "<p>Inference questions ask “Which choice most logically completes the text?” or “What can most reasonably be inferred from the text?” They are often the hardest R&amp;W questions because the answer is not literally written down. There is a specific, learnable logic to them, and it starts with what “inference” means on this test.</p>",
    parts: [
      {"title": "Part 1 · The core idea", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<p>An inference is not a guess and not a creative reading. On the SAT it is <b>a small logical step beyond what the text says, forced by what the text says.</b> The standard is “must be true given the passage”. The right answer usually feels almost boring: a modest conclusion.</p>\n<p>This goes against everyday reading, where interesting interpretations feel smart. Here the most interesting interpretation is usually the trap, because it needs assumptions the text never gave you. Stay close to the text and prefer the least surprising answer that still follows.</p>\n<p>The text gives you premises, and the answer is the conclusion that has to follow. If two reasonable people could disagree about whether it follows, it is speculation.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 2 · The method", "mins": "about 6 min", "html": "<ol>\n<li><b>Read actively and note the key facts.</b> Who did what, when, and with what result. Science passages build a small structure: a phenomenon, a finding, then a puzzle or qualification. The question hinges on one seam of that structure.</li>\n<li><b>Read the sentence with the blank carefully.</b> The final sentence is half-built, and the blank must finish its logic. Signal words tell you the blank's role:\n  <ul>\n  <li>“These results suggest…” needs a conclusion, not a restatement of the results.</li>\n  <li>“This is significant because…” needs a reason why it matters.</li>\n  <li>“However…” or “though…” needs a qualification.</li>\n  </ul></li>\n<li><b>Predict before looking at the choices.</b> Even a rough prediction (“something about why the method works better in labs”) keeps you in the text's logic and away from whichever choice sounds intelligent.</li>\n<li><b>Apply the must-be-true test.</b> Ask of each choice: could this be false while everything in the passage stays true? If yes, eliminate it.</li>\n</ol>"},
      {"title": "Part 3 · The four ways choices overshoot", "mins": "about 5 min", "html": "<ul>\n<li><b>Too strong.</b> The text says the treatment “reduced symptoms in most patients”; the choice says it “cures the condition”. Words like <i>all, always, never, guarantees, proves</i> are red flags. The right answer usually uses calibrated language: <i>may, tends to, often, suggests</i>. When two choices are otherwise equal, the more modest one is more likely correct.</li>\n<li><b>Causal leap.</b> The text says two things happen together, or one came first; the choice says one caused the other. This is the most common overshoot in science passages.</li>\n<li><b>New information.</b> The choice introduces a fact, group or comparison the text never mentions. It may be true in the real world; it is not an inference from this text.</li>\n<li><b>Wrong direction or wrong scope.</b> The text says the policy failed in cities; the choice says it failed in rural areas too. Or the text says results were mixed and the choice says they were uniformly negative.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-mistake\"><b>Common mistake.</b> Treating “not mentioned” as “denied”. If the passage never says the new method works in humans, the inference is not “the method doesn't work in humans”. Absence of evidence is not negative evidence. The safe inference is whatever the text did establish.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 4 · Worked example", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<blockquote>In a three-year study, volunteers who replaced one hour of daily screen time with walking reported better sleep. The researchers note, however, that participants knew which habit they were practicing. These findings are promising, though ______<br><br>\n<b>Question.</b> Which choice most logically completes the text?</blockquote>\n<p><b>Prediction.</b> “Though” calls for a qualification about the study's reliability, and the “knew which habit” detail was placed there to be used. Something like: the results may partly reflect participants' expectations, not walking itself.</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Choice</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>A) walking is the most effective known intervention for sleep.</td><td>Too strong. “Most effective known” is supported nowhere.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>B) the improvement may be partly due to participants' expectations.</b></td><td><b>Correct.</b> Matches the prediction, uses the planted detail, calibrated language.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>C) participants who slept poorly should not walk.</td><td>New direction. The text makes no recommendations.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D) screen time causes poor sleep in the general population.</td><td>Causal leap plus scope extension: volunteers are not everyone.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>B is the least dramatic choice. That is the pattern: on inference questions, modest wins.</p>"}
    ]
  },
  "U14": {
    lead: "<p>Now we switch to grammar, and this is the highest-yield place to start. Sentence boundary questions appear in every module, and their rules are fully reliable. Once you know them, these are some of the fastest points on the test.</p>",
    parts: [
      {"title": "Part 1 · The core idea", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<p>The whole topic rests on one distinction: a <b>complete sentence</b> (an independent clause) versus an <b>incomplete</b> one (a fragment or dependent clause).</p>\n<p>A complete sentence needs a subject and a verb that together express a finished thought. “The scientist published her findings.” Subject: scientist. Verb: published. It can stand alone.</p>\n<p>A fragment is missing a piece, or has a word that stops it standing alone. “Because the results were surprising” has a subject and a verb, but <i>because</i> leaves it waiting for the rest. “Having analyzed the data” has no working verb.</p>\n<p>The words that turn a complete thought into a fragment are the <b>subordinators</b>: <i>because, although, while, since, when, if, after, before, unless, whereas</i>. A word group that starts with one of these cannot stand alone.</p>\n<p>Every boundary question tests this. Each punctuation mark has rules about what it may join. Once you can tell sentences from fragments, the punctuation follows mechanically.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 2 · The connector menu", "mins": "about 6 min", "html": "<p>There are four ways to join two complete sentences, and the SAT tests all four.</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Connector</th><th>Example</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Period</td><td>The experiment failed. The team revised the protocol.</td><td>Works between any two complete sentences.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Semicolon</td><td>The experiment failed; the team revised the protocol.</td><td>Both sides must be complete sentences.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Comma + FANBOYS<br>(for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so)</td><td>The experiment failed, so the team revised the protocol.</td><td>A comma alone does not join two sentences.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Semicolon + transition + comma</td><td>The experiment failed; however, the team revised the protocol.</td><td><i>However, therefore, moreover, nevertheless, consequently</i> are adverbs. They cannot join sentences alone.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>“The experiment failed, however, the team revised the protocol” is a comma splice, one of the test's favourite traps.</p>\n<p><b>The colon.</b> A colon needs a complete sentence <i>before</i> it. What follows can be a list, an explanation, or another sentence: “The team changed one thing: the temperature of the sample.” If the words before the colon cannot stand alone (“The team changed: the temperature”), the colon is wrong.</p>\n<p><b>Joining a fragment to a sentence.</b> When a dependent clause comes first, a comma follows it: “Although the results were surprising, the team published.” When it comes second, there is usually no comma: “The team published although the results were surprising.”</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 3 · The error catalog", "mins": "about 5 min", "html": "<p>Every boundary question contains one of these errors. Learn to name it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Comma splice:</b> two complete sentences joined by only a comma. “The results were surprising, the team published anyway.” Fix: period, semicolon, or comma + FANBOYS.</li>\n<li><b>Run-on (fused sentence):</b> two complete sentences with nothing between them. “The results were surprising the team published anyway.” Same three fixes.</li>\n<li><b>Fragment treated as a sentence:</b> a period or semicolon with a fragment on one side. “The results were surprising; because the equipment had malfunctioned.” Fix: remove the semicolon and attach the fragment to the sentence.</li>\n<li><b>Missing comma after an introductory clause:</b> “Because the storm arrived early the game was canceled” needs a comma after “early”.</li>\n<li><b>The “however” splice:</b> “, however,” joining two sentences. The fix is a semicolon or period before “however”.</li>\n</ul>"},
      {"title": "Part 4 · The method", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<p>The four choices differ only in punctuation. Follow this procedure:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Find the seam.</b> On each side of the punctuation, ask: complete sentence or fragment? Check for a subject and a working verb. A subordinator (<i>because, although, while</i>) means that side is a fragment.</li>\n<li><b>Decide what is needed.</b> Sentence + sentence needs a legal sentence connector: period, semicolon, comma + FANBOYS, or semicolon + transition. Sentence + fragment needs at most a comma.</li>\n<li><b>Test the choices.</b> Does the punctuation match what the two sides need? Wrong choices fail in nameable ways: comma splice, fused sentence, fragment left standing alone, transition word with only a comma.</li>\n</ol>\n<blockquote><b>Example.</b> The orchestra rehearsed daily, moreover, the conductor added new pieces to the program.\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Left: “The orchestra rehearsed daily” → complete</div><div>Right: “the conductor added new pieces to the program” → complete</div><div>Needed: a sentence connector. Comma + “moreover” is not one.</div><div>Fix: “…rehearsed daily; moreover, the conductor…” or a period.</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>The wrong choices will be the comma splice, a fused version, or a version that turns one side into a fragment.</p>"}
    ]
  },
  "U15": {
    lead: "<p>Part 1 of Problem-Solving and Data Analysis: ratios, unit rates, proportions and percentages. The domain rewards organization more than algebra, and this material is mostly arithmetic with structure. The multiplier idea here is the same one behind the exponential models in Class 12.</p>",
    parts: [
      {"title": "Part 1 · The core idea", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<p>A <b>ratio</b> compares two quantities by multiplication: 3 to 2 means for every 3 of one thing there are 2 of the other. A <b>rate</b> is a ratio with different units (miles per hour). A <b>percentage</b> is a rate per hundred.</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>The three percentage questions</th><th>Compute</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>What is <i>p</i>% of <i>n</i>?</td><td>(<i>p</i> / 100) × <i>n</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>What percent is <i>a</i> of <i>b</i>?</td><td>(<i>a</i> / <i>b</i>) × 100</td></tr>\n<tr><td><i>a</i> is <i>p</i>% of what?</td><td><i>a</i> ÷ (<i>p</i> / 100)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>"},
      {"title": "Part 2 · The percent multiplier method", "mins": "about 6 min", "html": "<p>Every percentage question runs through one idea: <b>a percent change is multiplication by a factor.</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increase by 15% → multiply by 1.15</li>\n<li>Decrease by 15% → multiply by 0.85</li>\n<li>Successive changes multiply: +10% then −10% is 1.10 × 0.90 = 0.99, a net 1% <i>decrease</i>. Consecutive percent changes do not add.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Percent change</b> = (new − old) / old × 100. The denominator is always the <i>original</i>.</p>\n<blockquote><b>Example.</b> A $240 jacket is discounted 25%, then a 10% tax is applied to the discounted price. What is the final price?\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>240 × 0.75 = 180</div><div>180 × 1.10 = 198 → $198</div></div></blockquote>\n<p><b>Percent greater than, percent less than.</b> “A is 50% more than B” means A = 1.5B. “A is 40% less than B” means A = 0.6B. The trap: <b>the relation is not symmetric.</b> If A is 25% less than B, then A = 0.75B, so B = A / 0.75 ≈ 1.333A, which is 33⅓% more than A. “A is 25% less than B” does not mean “B is 25% more than A”. Write both in terms of one variable and compare.</p>\n<blockquote><b>Reverse percentage.</b> After a 20% discount and a 6% tax, the final price was $127.20. What was the original price?\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>original × 0.80 × 1.06 = 127.20</div><div>original = 127.20 / (0.80 × 1.06) = 127.20 / 0.848 = 150 → $150</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>Do not combine the percents by adding or subtracting. A 20% discount and a 6% tax are not a 14% discount. Multiplying the factors is the only correct chain.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 3 · Ratios and proportions", "mins": "about 5 min", "html": "<p><b>Ratios with totals.</b> “The ratio of cats to dogs is 3:2 and there are 40 animals in total.” Total parts = 3 + 2 = 5, so one part = 8. Cats = 24, dogs = 16. The parts method is faster than algebra.</p>\n<p><b>Direct or inverse?</b> Decide the direction before setting anything up.</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Direction</th><th>Set-up</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Same direction: more of one means more of the other</td><td><i>a</i>/<i>b</i> = <i>c</i>/<i>d</i>, then cross-multiply</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Opposite direction: more of one means less of the other</td><td>The product stays constant</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>5 machines take 7 hours. How long do 7 machines take?\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>More machines, less time → inverse → machines × hours is constant</div><div>5 × 7 = 35 → 35 / 7 = 5 hours</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>Mislabelling the direction is the main error here.</p>\n<p><b>Unit rates.</b> “12 ounces cost $3.60. What is the price per pound?” 3.60 / 12 = $0.30 per ounce, then 0.30 × 16 = $4.80 per pound. Going from per-ounce to per-pound means multiplying by 16, and the SAT sets its trap at exactly this step.</p>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-mistake\"><b>Common mistake.</b> Unit errors in compound rates. To turn 90 km per hour into a per-second rate, dividing by 60 once is not enough: hours → minutes → seconds is two divisions, and a missing factor changes the answer by 60. Write the units as fractions and cancel them like numbers: km/h × h/min × min/s. Hours cancel, minutes cancel, and 90 ÷ 3600 = 0.025 km per second, or 25 metres per second.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 4 · The Desmos angle", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<ul>\n<li><b>Percent chains</b> are one line: type 240 × 0.75 × 1.10.</li>\n<li><b>Reverse percentage:</b> compute 127.20 / (0.8 × 1.06) directly, or graph <i>y</i> = <i>x</i> × 0.8 × 1.06 and <i>y</i> = 127.20 and read the intersection.</li>\n<li><b>Proportions</b> are faster by cross-multiplication than in Desmos.</li>\n<li><b>Unit conversions with awkward factors</b> (such as 3.785 L per gallon): multiply through the conversion factors in one line.</li>\n</ul>"}
    ]
  },
  "U16": {
    lead: "<p>Class 14 built the foundation: sentence versus fragment, and the four legal ways to connect sentences. This class adds the remaining punctuation tools and the trap patterns the SAT builds by combining rules. By the end, boundary questions should feel like checking a set of known conditions.</p>",
    parts: [
      {"title": "Part 1 · The core idea", "mins": "about 2 min", "html": "<p>The rules from Class 14 plus today's tools form a complete system. Every boundary question can be answered with the sentence-or-fragment check and one of these tools. There is no hidden rule. The test reuses the same few situations with different words, so the skill is pattern recognition.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 2 · New tools", "mins": "about 6 min", "html": "<p><b>Dash pairs.</b> Two dashes wrap an aside inside a sentence, the way two commas or parentheses can.</p>\n<blockquote>The theory—first proposed in a 1911 paper—remains influential.</blockquote>\n<p>Two rules:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>The pair must be complete and must match.</b> If the aside opens with a dash, it closes with a dash. If it opens with a comma, it closes with a comma. A dash on one side and a comma on the other is wrong.</li>\n<li><b>The pair wraps exactly the aside.</b> Remove everything between the marks and the sentence must still read correctly: “The theory remains influential.” The trap choice puts the closing dash too far along, so part of the main sentence ends up inside the aside.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Single dash.</b> One dash works like a colon: it follows a complete sentence and introduces an explanation or emphasis. “The team had one goal—victory.”</p>\n<p><b>Parentheses</b> work like a dash pair for a quieter aside: “The results (published in 2019) were consistent.” The same rule applies: open and close.</p>\n<p><b>Essential or non-essential?</b> Before wrapping anything in commas or dashes, decide whether the sentence needs it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Non-essential:</b> extra information. Remove it and the sentence still says who or what is meant. It takes a pair of commas, dashes or parentheses.</li>\n<li><b>Essential:</b> the information identifies who or what is meant. Remove it and the sentence becomes unclear. It takes <b>no punctuation</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Sentence</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Toni Morrison, an American novelist, won the Nobel Prize in 1993.</td><td>Non-essential. “Toni Morrison won the Nobel Prize in 1993” is complete and clear.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>American novelist Toni Morrison won the Nobel Prize in 1993.</td><td>Essential. A title or description before a name takes no commas: “American novelist won the Nobel Prize” does not work.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The study that tracked 5,000 patients was published in 2019.</td><td>Essential. The “that” clause says which study, so no commas.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The study, which tracked 5,000 patients, was published in 2019.</td><td>Non-essential. A “which” clause adds a detail and takes commas.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>The test:</b> delete the phrase. If the sentence still makes sense and still points to the same person or thing, the phrase is non-essential and needs punctuation on both sides. If not, it needs none.</p>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-info\"><b>No punctuation can be the answer.</b> Do not put a comma between a subject and its verb, between a verb and its object, or before a prepositional phrase. When one choice has no punctuation at all, check it first: it is often correct.</p>\n<p><b>Colons.</b> A colon must follow a complete sentence. What comes after it can be a list, a definition, or a sentence that explains.</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Sentence</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>The team had one goal: victory.</td><td>Correct. “The team had one goal” is complete.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The results were clear: the hypothesis was confirmed.</td><td>Correct. A sentence explains a sentence.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The reason for the delay was: the storm.</td><td>Wrong. “The reason for the delay was” cannot stand alone.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Possessives, a preview.</b> Boundary questions sometimes hide a possessive inside a choice. “The team's schedule” is one team. “The teams' schedules” is several teams. Class 20 covers this fully.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 3 · The trap patterns", "mins": "about 5 min", "html": "<p><b>Lists after a colon.</b> Commas separate the items. “The study had two phases: a field survey and a lab analysis.” Semicolons separate the items only when the items contain commas themselves: “We visited Paris, France; Rome, Italy; and London, England.”</p>\n<p><b>The transition-word splice.</b> The most-tested trap from Class 14, worth repeating. <i>However, moreover, therefore, consequently, nevertheless, instead, meanwhile, likewise</i> are adverbs, not conjunctions. Between two sentences they need a semicolon or a period before them. “, however,” between two complete sentences is a comma splice.</p>\n<p><b>Which side does the transition belong to?</b> Sometimes the choices move the punctuation around the transition: “…the first trial; however, the second…” versus “…the first trial, however; the second…”. Both are punctuated legally. Read the meaning: does “however” contrast with what came before, or does it belong to the end of the first sentence? Put the semicolon where the contrast starts.</p>\n<p><b>Period versus semicolon.</b> Between two complete sentences these do the same job. So if two choices differ <i>only</i> in period versus semicolon, neither can be the answer, because the SAT has one correct choice. Eliminate both and look at the other two. If the two choices differ in something else as well (a word such as <i>its</i> / <i>it's</i>, or a tense), that other difference decides.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 4 · Worked example", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<blockquote>The first documented use of a compass in navigation dates to around 1119 in ______ texts describe its spread along trade routes.</blockquote>\n<p><b>Check the sides.</b> “The first documented use… dates to around 1119 in China” is complete. “Later texts describe its spread along trade routes” is complete. Sentence + sentence needs a sentence connector.</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Choice</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>A) China later</td><td>Run-on. Nothing joins the two sentences.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>B) China, later</td><td>Comma splice.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>C) China; later</b></td><td><b>Correct.</b> A semicolon with a complete sentence on each side.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D) China and later</td><td>A FANBOYS word joining two sentences needs a comma before it.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Name the error in each wrong choice and the answer is what remains.</p>"}
    ]
  },
  "U17": {
    lead: "<p>Part 2 of Problem-Solving and Data Analysis covers the statistics side: mean, median and range, data displays and standard deviation, probability from two-way tables, scatterplots and lines of fit, and the survey questions the SAT loves: margin of error, generalizability and study design. These questions look wordy, but they are among the most rule-based on the test.</p>",
    parts: [
      {"title": "Part 1 · Mean, median, and range", "mins": "about 6 min", "html": "<p><b>Mean</b> = sum divided by count. <b>Median</b> = the middle value when sorted, or the average of the two middle values for an even count. <b>Range</b> = maximum minus minimum.</p>\n<p>The SAT's favourite questions ask what happens when the data changes.</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Change</th><th>Mean</th><th>Median</th><th>Range</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Add 5 to every value</td><td>+5</td><td>+5</td><td>Same</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Multiply every value by 3</td><td>×3</td><td>×3</td><td>×3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Raise one value by 10, in a set of 20</td><td>+10/20 = +0.5</td><td>Depends on position</td><td>Depends on position</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Changing one value</b> changes the mean by (change) / <i>n</i>. The same logic answers “which value of <i>x</i> makes the mean equal <i>K</i>?”: the sum needed is <i>K</i> × <i>n</i>, then subtract the known sum.</p>\n<p><b>Mean versus median.</b> Outliers on the high end (a few huge salaries among modest ones) pull the mean up more than the median, so mean &gt; median. The median resists outliers because it depends only on position. “Which measure best represents the typical value?” when an outlier exists → the median.</p>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-mistake\"><b>Common mistake.</b> Forgetting to sort before taking the median. The median of 3, 1, 9, 4 is not the average of 1 and 9. Sort first: 1, 3, 4, 9, so the median is (3 + 4) / 2 = 3.5. The values arrive unsorted on purpose.</p>\n<p><b>Frequency tables.</b> When data comes as value × how many times, the mean is the sum of (value × count) divided by the total count. For the median, walk up the cumulative counts until you reach the halfway position. With 40 values the median sits between the 20th and 21st.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 2 · Data displays and standard deviation", "mins": "about 5 min", "html": "<ul>\n<li><b>Dot plots and histograms</b> show the distribution: how many values fall at each point or in each interval. The most common value (the mode) is at the peak, where the dot stack or bar is highest. To find the median, count along to the middle position, as with a frequency table.</li>\n<li><b>Box plots</b> show the five-number summary: minimum, Q1, median, Q3, maximum. The box runs from Q1 to Q3. Its length is the <b>interquartile range</b> (IQR = Q3 − Q1), which holds the middle 50% of the data. A box plot shows the median but not the mean.</li>\n<li><b>Standard deviation</b> measures how spread out the values are from the mean. Data clustered tightly near the mean has a low standard deviation. Data spread toward the extremes has a high one.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The SAT never asks you to calculate a standard deviation. It asks you to compare two data sets: the one whose values sit farther from its center has the larger standard deviation. Adding the same number to every value does not change it; multiplying every value does.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 3 · Probability and two-way tables", "mins": "about 5 min", "html": "<p>A <b>two-way table</b> shows how two categories overlap. Fill in or check the Total row and column first, so you do not misread the table.</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th></th><th>Bus</th><th>Car</th><th>Total</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Grade 9</td><td>30</td><td>20</td><td>50</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Grade 10</td><td>25</td><td>45</td><td>70</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Total</td><td>55</td><td>65</td><td>120</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Probability</b> = number of cases you want ÷ number of cases possible. The probability that a student chosen at random takes the bus is 55/120.</p>\n<p><b>Conditional probability.</b> The probability of A given B is (number of A and B) ÷ (total of B). The “given” condition shrinks the group you are choosing from.</p>\n<blockquote>A Grade 9 student is chosen at random. What is the probability the student takes the bus?\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Given: Grade 9 → use the Grade 9 row only → 30 / 50 = 0.6</div></div></blockquote>\n<blockquote>A student who takes the bus is chosen at random. What is the probability the student is in Grade 9?\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Given: bus → use the Bus column only → 30 / 55</div></div></blockquote>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-mistake\"><b>Trap.</b> Do not divide by the grand total for a conditional probability. Use the total of the row or column named by the “given” condition. Here 30/120 is the wrong answer to both questions, and the two questions have different answers because the condition is different.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 4 · Scatterplots and lines of fit", "mins": "about 5 min", "html": "<p><b>Direction.</b> Positive association (both rise together), negative (one rises as the other falls), or no association (a cloud).</p>\n<p><b>Line of best fit, <i>y</i> = <i>mx</i> + <i>b</i>.</b> The slope <i>m</i> is the predicted change in <i>y</i> per unit of <i>x</i>, in context: “each additional year of experience predicts an increase of $2,300 in salary.” The intercept <i>b</i> is the predicted <i>y</i> when <i>x</i> = 0. If <i>x</i> = 0 is outside the data, the right choice describes a prediction and does not claim it was observed.</p>\n<p><b>Residuals.</b> Residual = actual − predicted. A point above the line means the model underpredicted that case. If the line predicts 8.5 at <i>x</i> = 4 and the point is (4, 9.5), the residual is 1. “Which point has the largest residual?” means the point farthest from the line vertically.</p>\n<p><b>Reading values.</b> Interpolation (predicting inside the data range) is safe. Extrapolation (beyond it) is not. The <i>least</i> reliable prediction is the one farthest outside the plotted data.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 5 · Margin of error, generalizability, and study design", "mins": "about 6 min", "html": "<p>These topics use almost no formulas, and each answers a different question.</p>\n<p><b>Generalizability:</b> can the findings extend to the whole population? This depends entirely on <b>how the sample was chosen</b>. A random sample of the population of interest generalizes. A non-random sample (volunteers, people at one location) does not, however large.</p>\n<blockquote>A researcher surveyed 5,000 visitors to the aquarium. Can the results be generalized to all city residents?\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>No. Aquarium visitors are not a random sample of residents.</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>Sample size affects <i>precision</i>, not representativeness.</p>\n<p><b>Margin of error</b> gives a range for the true population value. If 58% of a random sample support a policy with a margin of error of ±4%, the true percentage is estimated to be between 54% and 62%. For “which value is plausible?”, check whether each candidate falls inside the interval.</p>\n<p><b>Larger samples give smaller margins of error.</b> If a question asks which change would decrease the margin of error, the answer is increasing the sample size.</p>\n<p><b>Study design.</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Observational study:</b> researchers observe without interfering. It can show an association. It cannot show cause and effect.</li>\n<li><b>Experiment:</b> researchers use <b>random assignment</b> to a treatment group and a control group. Only an experiment with random assignment supports a claim of cause and effect.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Keep the two kinds of randomness apart. Random <i>sampling</i> decides who the results apply to. Random <i>assignment</i> decides whether you can say one thing caused another.</p>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-tip\"><b>Tip.</b> The questions have different triggers. “Can the results be generalized to all…” points to the sampling method. “Margin of error” or “likely between” points to the interval arithmetic. “Caused”, “leads to” or “results in” points to random assignment. Answering a generalization question with “the sample was too small” is the classic wrong move when the real issue is how the sample was picked.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 6 · The Desmos angle", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<ul>\n<li><b>Mean and median from a list:</b> enter the data in a table and type <code>mean(x_1)</code> and <code>median(x_1)</code>.</li>\n<li><b>Missing value for a target mean:</b> use the sum logic, or type (known sum + <i>x</i>) / <i>n</i> and the target as two expressions and read the intersection.</li>\n<li><b>Line of best fit:</b> enter the points in a table, type <code>y_1~mx_1+b</code>, then read <i>m</i> and <i>b</i>, or define <code>f(x)=mx+b</code> to evaluate predictions.</li>\n<li><b>Probability:</b> type the fraction from the two-way table, such as 30/55, to get the decimal when the choices are decimals or percents.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Statistics questions on the SAT are mostly reasoning, so Desmos is a helper here. The mean-change and interval logic should be done in your head.</p>"}
    ]
  },
  "U18": {
    lead: "<p>This class covers linear inequalities: solving them, systems of them, and graphing them with shading. On the Digital SAT they appear in two shapes: solving an inequality (which value satisfies it?) and “which graph or point fits this system of inequalities?”, where Desmos saves serious time.</p>",
    parts: [
      {"title": "Part 1 · The core idea", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<p>An inequality looks like 3<i>x</i> + 5 &lt; 20. You solve it almost like a linear equation from Class 3: do the same thing to both sides to isolate <i>x</i>. There is <b>one key difference</b>: when you multiply or divide both sides by a negative number, the sign flips.</p>\n<blockquote><div class=\"lw-work\"><div>−2x &gt; 10 → divide by −2 → x &lt; −5</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>Forgetting the flip is the most common error in this topic. The reason: multiplying by a negative reverses the number line, so what was larger becomes smaller. Positive multipliers keep the order; negative ones reverse it.</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Words</th><th>Symbol</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>no more than, at most</td><td>≤</td></tr>\n<tr><td>at least, no fewer than</td><td>≥</td></tr>\n<tr><td>more than</td><td>&gt; (strict)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>less than, fewer than</td><td>&lt; (strict)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>If a question asks “which value satisfies the inequality?”, substitute each choice and check. It is often faster than solving.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 2 · Systems of inequalities", "mins": "about 5 min", "html": "<p>A system like <i>y</i> &gt; 2<i>x</i> − 1 and <i>y</i> ≤ −<i>x</i> + 4 describes a <b>region of the plane</b>: the points that satisfy both conditions at once. Each inequality is a half-plane, one side of a line, and the solution is where the two overlap.</p>\n<p>Three drawing rules:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Strict signs (&gt;, &lt;) get a <b>dashed</b> boundary line. The boundary is excluded.</li>\n<li>Non-strict signs (≥, ≤) get a <b>solid</b> line. The boundary is included.</li>\n<li>To choose the side to shade, plug in a test point, usually (0, 0). If it satisfies the inequality, shade its side. If the line passes through (0, 0), use another point such as (1, 0).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The most common question is “which point (<i>x</i>, <i>y</i>) is a solution to the system?” Substitute each pair into <b>both</b> inequalities. The pair that satisfies both is the answer. With small numbers this is faster than graphing.</p>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-mistake\"><b>Common mistake.</b> Checking a point against only one inequality. It must satisfy both. The “half-solution” is exactly the trap answer the test builds.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 3 · The Desmos method", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<p>Desmos accepts inequalities exactly as written.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Type <code>y&gt;2x-1</code> and Desmos shades the correct half-plane with a dashed boundary.</li>\n<li>Type the second inequality. The doubly shaded region is the solution set.</li>\n<li>For “which point is a solution?”, plot the candidate points. The one inside the doubly shaded region is the answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is much faster than graphing by hand, especially with general form like <i>Ax</i> + <i>By</i> ≤ <i>C</i>. Desmos takes it as written, while solving for <i>y</i> by hand means dividing by <i>B</i> and risking a missed sign flip when <i>B</i> is negative.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 4 · Inequalities in context", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<p>Word problems translate with the Class 3 vocabulary, plus a common-sense rule: numbers of items cannot be negative, and usually must be whole.</p>\n<blockquote>Adult tickets cost $12 and child tickets cost $7, and at most $100 was spent.\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>12a + 7c ≤ 100, with a ≥ 0 and c ≥ 0</div><div>Maximum number of adult tickets: 12a ≤ 100 → a = 8</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>Another pattern: “which system of inequalities represents the situation?” Choose the right signs (“no more than” versus “less than”) and match each number to the right variable. Testing a scenario you know is allowed, such as <i>a</i> = 0 and <i>c</i> = 0, quickly eliminates choices with the wrong signs.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 5 · Absolute value: inequalities and equations", "mins": "about 5 min", "html": "<p>The absolute value |<i>x</i>| is the distance of <i>x</i> from zero, so it is never negative.</p>\n<p><b>Absolute value inequalities</b> are range constraints.</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Inequality</th><th>Means</th><th>Picture</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>|<i>x</i>| &lt; <i>a</i></td><td>−<i>a</i> &lt; <i>x</i> &lt; <i>a</i></td><td><i>x</i> is trapped between −<i>a</i> and <i>a</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>|<i>x</i>| &gt; <i>a</i></td><td><i>x</i> &gt; <i>a</i> or <i>x</i> &lt; −<i>a</i></td><td><i>x</i> is pushed out beyond <i>a</i> or −<i>a</i></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>How to solve</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>For |<i>Ax</i> + <i>B</i>| &lt; <i>C</i>:</b> solve the compound inequality −<i>C</i> &lt; <i>Ax</i> + <i>B</i> &lt; <i>C</i> by isolating <i>x</i> in the middle.</li>\n<li><b>For |<i>Ax</i> + <i>B</i>| &gt; <i>C</i>:</b> solve two separate inequalities, <i>Ax</i> + <i>B</i> &gt; <i>C</i> or <i>Ax</i> + <i>B</i> &lt; −<i>C</i>.</li>\n</ol>\n<blockquote>|2<i>x</i> − 3| &lt; 5\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>−5 &lt; 2x − 3 &lt; 5 → add 3 → −2 &lt; 2x &lt; 8 → divide by 2 → −1 &lt; x &lt; 4</div></div></blockquote>\n<blockquote>|2<i>x</i> − 3| &gt; 5\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>2x − 3 &gt; 5 → x &gt; 4</div><div>or 2x − 3 &lt; −5 → x &lt; −1</div></div></blockquote>\n<p><b>Absolute value equations.</b> To solve |<i>Ax</i> + <i>B</i>| = <i>C</i> you must consider two cases, because the inside can be positive or negative.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Case 1:</b> <i>Ax</i> + <i>B</i> = <i>C</i></li>\n<li><b>Case 2:</b> <i>Ax</i> + <i>B</i> = −<i>C</i></li>\n</ol>\n<blockquote>|2<i>x</i> − 3| = 5\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Case 1: 2x − 3 = 5 → 2x = 8 → x = 4</div><div>Case 2: 2x − 3 = −5 → 2x = −2 → x = −1</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>An absolute value is never negative, so |<i>Ax</i> + <i>B</i>| = −2 has no solution. If the question asks for the sum or product of the solutions, find both first: here the sum is 4 + (−1) = 3.</p>\n<p><b>Desmos shortcut.</b> Type the inequality or equation as it appears, for example <code>abs(2x-3)&lt;5</code> or <code>abs(2x-3)=5</code>. Desmos shades the solution of an inequality, and for an equation it draws a vertical line at each solution.</p>"}
    ]
  },
  "U19": {
    lead: "<p>We move from punctuation to the words themselves. Form, Structure, and Sense covers four word-level error types: verb forms, pronouns that point to the wrong noun, modifiers in the wrong place, and apostrophes that mark possession wrongly. Verbs are the most-tested category in the group, so they come first. All four reward one habit: identify exactly who or what a word refers to before deciding.</p>",
    parts: [
      {"title": "Part 1 · Verbs: the core idea", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<p><b>Agreement:</b> does the verb match its subject in number? One dog barks; many dogs bark. “The list of items <i>is</i> on the desk” (one list), not “are”.</p>\n<p><b>Tense:</b> does the verb's time match the sentence's timeline? “She walked to school yesterday.” “She walks daily.” “She will walk tomorrow.”</p>\n<p>Both checks are mechanical. You find the subject, count it, and check the time words. The difficulty is all in the disguises: long sentences that bury the subject far from the verb, and time words that seem to point one way while the sentence means another.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 2 · Subject–verb agreement", "mins": "about 6 min", "html": "<p><b>Find the true subject.</b> It is the thing doing the verb, and it can be far away. Strip out everything that is not the core: prepositional phrases, inserted clauses, appositives.</p>\n<blockquote>The collection of rare stamps is valuable.\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Remove “of rare stamps” → The collection is valuable → singular</div></div></blockquote>\n<blockquote>The scientists who discovered the isotope were celebrated.\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>The verb matches “scientists”, not “isotope” → plural</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>Common disguises:</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Disguise</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>A phrase between subject and verb</td><td>The box of chocolates <b>is</b>…</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A clause between subject and verb</td><td>The paintings that were auctioned <b>were</b>…</td></tr>\n<tr><td><i>Each, every, either, neither</i>: always singular</td><td>Each of the students <b>has</b> a locker. Neither of the answers <b>is</b> correct.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><i>There is / There are</i>: the subject comes after the verb</td><td>There <b>are</b> a book and a lamp on the desk. Check by putting the subject first: A book and a lamp are on the desk.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Subjects joined by <i>and</i>: plural</td><td>The coach and the captain <b>are</b> ready.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Subjects joined by <i>or / nor</i>: match the nearer one</td><td>Neither the players nor the coach <b>was</b> ready.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-mistake\"><b>Common mistake.</b> Matching the verb to the nearest noun. “The quality of the recordings have improved” sounds fine because “recordings” is close, but the subject is “quality”, so it must be “has improved”. This is the most common agreement error on the test. Strip the sentence to its core before choosing.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 3 · Verb tense", "mins": "about 6 min", "html": "<p><b>Step 1: Anchor the timeline.</b> Find the time words: <i>yesterday, in 1928, currently, by the time she arrived, since 1990</i>. These are the sentence's clock.</p>\n<p><b>Step 2: Match the verb to the clock.</b></p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Tense</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>A finished time in the past</td><td>Simple past</td><td>In 1962, the team <b>published</b> its findings.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Started in the past, continues to now</td><td>Present perfect</td><td>The project <b>has grown</b> every year since 2015.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>An earlier past inside a past story</td><td>Past perfect</td><td>By the time the rescue team arrived, the fire <b>had spread</b>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>General truths and habits</td><td>Simple present</td><td>The moon <b>orbits</b> Earth.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>A sentence set in the past can still use the present for a fact that is always true: “Galileo showed that objects of different masses <b>fall</b> at the same rate.” His work was in the past; the fact is timeless.</p>\n<p><b>Step 3: With two verbs, keep their order consistent.</b> “When she arrived, the show had already started” (the starting came first). “When she arrives, the show will have started” (the future version of the same logic). The verb pair shows which event came first.</p>\n<p><b>Sometimes the question is about structure, not tense.</b> An -ing form cannot be the main verb of a sentence. “The team, analyzing the data, concluded…” is right. “The team analyzing the data.” is a fragment. If the sentence needs a working verb, do not pick a form that cannot do the job. This connects to the fragment rules in Class 14.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 4 · The verb method", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<ol>\n<li><b>Look at how the choices differ.</b> <i>is / are, was / were, has / have, study / studies, analyzing / analyzed.</i></li>\n<li><b>Find the true subject.</b> Strip the sentence. One or many?</li>\n<li><b>Check the clock.</b> Which time words anchor the sentence? Does the tense match?</li>\n<li><b>Eliminate by the two checks.</b> Most questions are settled by one check: three choices have the wrong agreement, or three have the wrong tense.</li>\n</ol>\n<blockquote><b>Example.</b> The discovery of the manuscripts, along with several letters, ______ the highlight of the exhibit. (was / were)\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Strip: The discovery … was the highlight</div><div>Subject: discovery → singular → was</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>The “along with” phrase is extra information, not part of the subject. Only <i>and</i> makes a subject plural.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 5 · Pronouns", "mins": "about 5 min", "html": "<p>A pronoun (<i>it, they, its, their, who, this</i>) stands in for a noun. The question is always: which noun does it point to, and does it match?</p>\n<p><b>Find the noun the pronoun replaces.</b> Ask “what is this ‘it’?” Look back into the previous sentence if needed. The SAT places two candidate nouns nearby, and one of them is wrong.</p>\n<blockquote>During the Renaissance, apprentices learned their trade by copying the work of masters. They often spent years perfecting a single technique.</blockquote>\n<p>“They” could be apprentices or masters; both are plural, so grammar cannot decide. The content does: the ones perfecting techniques are the apprentices. Fine as written.</p>\n<p><b>Number agreement.</b> Once you have the noun, check the count. “The committee announced <b>its</b> decision” (one committee). “The committee members announced <b>their</b> decisions” (many members). The favourite trap is a singular that sounds plural: <i>the team, the audience, the company</i> are each one thing, so “its” is correct. Students pick “their” by ear.</p>\n<p><b>Ambiguity.</b> Sometimes the sentence is grammatical but unclear, and the correct choice replaces the pronoun with the noun. “When Alice met Priya, she was carrying a heavy box” does not say who was carrying, so “Priya was carrying” beats “she”. Before choosing the spelled-out version, check that it matches what the passage means.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 6 · Modifiers", "mins": "about 5 min", "html": "<p>A modifier is a describing phrase. The rule is placement: <b>a describing phrase must sit next to the thing it describes.</b> When it does not, the sentence describes the wrong thing. These are misplaced or dangling modifiers.</p>\n<p><b>The test:</b> ask who or what is doing the action in the describing phrase. The answer must be the noun right next to it.</p>\n<blockquote>Walking through the museum, the paintings impressed Rosa.\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Who is walking? Rosa. But the phrase sits next to “the paintings”.</div><div>Fix: Walking through the museum, Rosa was impressed by the paintings.</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>The opening -ing phrase is the most common version. “Having analyzed the samples, the results showed…” Who analyzed? Not “the results”. The fix puts the doer right after the comma.</p>\n<p>Two more shapes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Opening past participles with a possessive trap.</b> “Discovered in 1891, the manuscript's pages were…” What was discovered? The manuscript. But the noun after the comma is “pages”, because “the manuscript's” is only describing them. The noun itself must follow: “Discovered in 1891, the manuscript…”</li>\n<li><b>Word order.</b> “Rosa almost attended every lecture” says she nearly attended and did not. “Rosa attended almost every lecture” says she attended most of them. Words like <i>only, nearly, almost</i> modify what comes right after them.</li>\n</ul>"},
      {"title": "Part 7 · Possessives and apostrophes", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<p>The apostrophe has two jobs: <b>possession</b> (the team's captain) and <b>contraction</b> (it's = it is). The tested error is confusing them.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Is it a contraction?</b> Expand it: <i>it's</i> = it is, <i>they're</i> = they are, <i>who's</i> = who is. If the sentence works with the expansion, it is a contraction. “It's clear that…” = “It is clear that…” (<i>Its'</i> is never a word.)</li>\n<li><b>If not, it is possession.</b> Apply the table below.</li>\n<li><b>Check the count.</b> Most questions come down to one owner or many. “The artist's most famous work” (one artist) versus “the artists' most famous works” (several). The sentence tells you how many owners there are.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Owner</th><th>Rule</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Singular</td><td>add 's</td><td>the student's essay</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Plural ending in s</td><td>apostrophe after the s</td><td>the students' essays</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Plural not ending in s</td><td>add 's</td><td>the children's books</td></tr>\n<tr><td>No owner, just a plural</td><td>no apostrophe</td><td>the students wrote essays</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Its versus it's.</b> “Its” with no apostrophe is possessive: “The city grew; its population doubled.” “It's” is “it is” or “it has”. The expansion test settles every case. The same goes for <i>their / they're</i> and <i>whose / who's</i>.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 8 · The unified method", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<p>Pronouns, modifiers and possessives share one question: <b>what does this word point to?</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A pronoun points to a noun. Find it, check the count, check for ambiguity.</li>\n<li>A modifier points to the doer of its action. Find who or what that is, check that it sits next to the phrase.</li>\n<li>An apostrophe points to an owner. Find the owner, check the count.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If a question seems to test one of these but the checks pass, recheck the other two, and the verb checks from Parts 1–4. The test sometimes hides a verb error inside the choice that fixes the pronoun.</p>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-tip\"><b>Tip.</b> A choice that fixes one error and introduces another is wrong. The winning choice is fully correct, so check every word in a choice, not just the one you expected to change.</p>"}
    ]
  },
  "U20": {
    lead: "<p>Geometry and Trigonometry is about 15% of the Math section, so it gets two classes. This one covers the reference sheet, angle rules, similar triangles, and area and volume. Right triangles, trigonometry and circles come in Class 24. The Digital SAT is consistent in this domain: you are not expected to memorize every formula, but you are expected to know when to use the reference sheet and how to work with shapes.</p>",
    parts: [
      {"title": "Part 1 · The reference sheet", "mins": "about 2 min", "html": "<p>The reference sheet in Bluebook gives you:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Area and circumference:</b> circle, rectangle, triangle.</li>\n<li><b>Pythagorean theorem:</b> <i>a</i>² + <i>b</i>² = <i>c</i>².</li>\n<li><b>Special right triangles:</b> the 30-60-90 and 45-45-90 side ratios.</li>\n<li><b>Volume:</b> rectangular prism, cylinder, sphere, cone, pyramid.</li>\n<li><b>Facts:</b> a circle has 360° or 2π radians; the angles of a triangle add to 180°.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-tip\"><b>Tip.</b> Do not compute an area or volume from memory. Take the formula from the reference sheet, so you never mix up 2π<i>r</i> (circumference) and π<i>r</i>² (area).</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 2 · Lines and angles", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>What it says</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Vertical angles</td><td>Angles opposite each other where two lines cross are equal.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Angles on a straight line</td><td>They add to 180°.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Parallel lines and a transversal</td><td>Corresponding angles are equal. Alternate interior angles are equal. Same-side interior angles add to 180°.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Exterior angle of a triangle</td><td>It equals the sum of the two interior angles that are not next to it.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Polygon angle sum</td><td>The interior angles of an <i>n</i>-sided polygon add to (<i>n</i> − 2) × 180°.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>With two parallel lines and a transversal there are only two angle sizes. All the acute angles are equal, all the obtuse angles are equal, and one of each adds to 180°.</p>\n<blockquote>Polygon angle sums\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Triangle: (3 − 2) × 180° = 180°</div><div>Quadrilateral: (4 − 2) × 180° = 360°</div><div>Hexagon: (6 − 2) × 180° = 720°, so each angle of a regular hexagon is 720° / 6 = 120°</div></div></blockquote>"},
      {"title": "Part 3 · Similar triangles", "mins": "about 5 min", "html": "<p><b>Similar triangles</b> have the same angles, even at different sizes, so their side lengths are <b>proportional</b>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If triangle A has sides 3, 4, 5 and triangle B is similar with a scale factor of 2, its sides are 6, 8, 10.</li>\n<li>Areas scale by the square of the factor: triangle B has 2² = 4 times the area of triangle A.</li>\n<li><b>The SAT trick:</b> similar triangles are hidden by nesting, a small triangle inside a larger one. Redraw them as two separate triangles to see the ratios.</li>\n</ul>"},
      {"title": "Part 4 · Area and scaling", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<p>Area questions are direct substitution from the reference sheet: rectangle = length × width, triangle = ½ × base × height, circle = π<i>r</i>².</p>\n<p><b>Scaling.</b> When every length of a shape is multiplied by <i>k</i>, the perimeter is multiplied by <i>k</i>, the area by <i>k</i>², and the volume by <i>k</i>³.</p>\n<blockquote>A square has side 5. Each side is tripled. What happens to the area?\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Scale factor 3 → area × 3² = 9</div><div>Check: 5² = 25 and 15² = 225 = 9 × 25</div></div></blockquote>"},
      {"title": "Part 5 · Volume", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<p>Volume questions are direct substitution if you use the reference sheet.</p>\n<p><b>The trap is units.</b> The question gives the radius in inches and asks for the volume in cubic feet. <b>Convert the lengths before you plug them into the formula.</b> Converting a radius is one step. Converting a finished volume means using the conversion factor three times (1 cubic foot = 12 × 12 × 12 cubic inches).</p>"}
    ]
  },
  "U21": {
    lead: "<p>Function notation looks intimidating, but a function is a machine that takes an input and gives an output. On the Digital SAT these questions are rarely abstract. They are about <b>interpreting what the machine does</b> in a real-world context.</p>",
    parts: [
      {"title": "Part 1 · The core idea", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<p>The notation <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) is a name for a rule.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><i>f</i> is the name of the rule.</li>\n<li><i>x</i> is the input, what you put in.</li>\n<li><i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) is the output, what the rule gives back.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><i>f</i>(3) = 10 says: <b>when the input is 3, the rule gives back 10.</b> On a graph, that is the point (3, 10).</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 2 · Evaluation and interpretation", "mins": "about 6 min", "html": "<p><b>1. Evaluation.</b> Replace every <i>x</i> with the input.</p>\n<blockquote>If <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = 2<i>x</i>² + 5, what is <i>f</i>(4)?\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>f(4) = 2(4)² + 5 = 2(16) + 5 = 37</div></div></blockquote>\n<p><b>2. Interpretation</b>, the most common type.</p>\n<blockquote>The function <i>P</i>(<i>t</i>) = 500(1.05)<sup><i>t</i></sup> models the population after <i>t</i> years. What does <i>P</i>(10) represent?\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>The population after 10 years.</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>Always check the labels. If <i>t</i> is in years, 10 means 10 years. If <i>P</i> is in thousands, the result is in thousands.</p>\n<p><b>3. Reading a graph.</b> On a graph of <i>y</i> = <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>), <i>f</i>(<i>a</i>) is the <b><i>y</i>-value</b> where <i>x</i> = <i>a</i>. To find <i>f</i>(2), find <i>x</i> = 2 on the horizontal axis, move up or down to the curve, and read the <i>y</i>-value.</p>\n<p>The reverse question, “for what value of <i>x</i> is <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = 2?”, starts from the vertical axis: find <i>y</i> = 2, move across to the curve, and read the <i>x</i>-value.</p>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-tip\"><b>Tip.</b> On a function question with a long word problem, look at the function before the text. What is the input, and what is the output? Write them down: if <i>x</i> is time, <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) is amount. That mapping often answers the question before you finish the paragraph.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 3 · Composite functions", "mins": "about 5 min", "html": "<p>A composite function is <i>f</i>(<i>g</i>(<i>x</i>)). It means: do <i>g</i> first, then put that result into <i>f</i>.</p>\n<blockquote><i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = <i>x</i> + 2 and <i>g</i>(<i>x</i>) = 3<i>x</i>. What is <i>f</i>(<i>g</i>(2))?\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Inside first: g(2) = 3(2) = 6</div><div>Then: f(6) = 6 + 2 = 8</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>On the SAT these are often hidden in tables.</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th><i>x</i></th><th><i>f</i>(<i>x</i>)</th><th><i>g</i>(<i>x</i>)</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>7</td><td>5</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3</td><td>9</td><td>1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5</td><td>12</td><td>3</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>What is <i>f</i>(<i>g</i>(1))? Find <i>g</i>(1) in the table: 5. Then find <i>f</i>(5): 12. The answer is 12. The trap answer is 7, which is <i>f</i>(1).</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 4 · Domain and range", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<ul>\n<li><b>Domain:</b> the set of all possible <b>inputs</b> (<i>x</i>-values). In context this is often “time cannot be negative”, so <i>x</i> ≥ 0.</li>\n<li><b>Range:</b> the set of all possible <b>outputs</b> (<i>y</i>-values). In context this is often “the population cannot be negative”, so <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) ≥ 0.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If a model is <i>f</i>(<i>t</i>) = 100(0.8)<sup><i>t</i></sup>, where <i>t</i> is the number of years since 2000, and it is now 2025, the domain is 0 ≤ <i>t</i> ≤ 25.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 5 · Function transformations", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<p>A transformation changes the position or shape of the graph of <i>y</i> = <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) without changing its basic structure.</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Change</th><th>Effect on the graph</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><i>f</i>(<i>x</i> − <i>h</i>)</td><td>Shifts <b>right</b> by <i>h</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td><i>f</i>(<i>x</i> + <i>h</i>)</td><td>Shifts <b>left</b> by <i>h</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td><i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) + <i>k</i></td><td>Shifts <b>up</b> by <i>k</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td><i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) − <i>k</i></td><td>Shifts <b>down</b> by <i>k</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>−<i>f</i>(<i>x</i>)</td><td>Reflects over the <i>x</i>-axis</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The horizontal shift is counter-intuitive: <i>x</i> − 3 moves the graph to the right. A change inside the brackets acts on <i>x</i> and goes the opposite way to its sign. A change outside acts on <i>y</i> and goes the way it looks.</p>\n<blockquote><i>g</i>(<i>x</i>) = <i>f</i>(<i>x</i> − 3) + 2\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>The whole graph moves right 3 and up 2</div><div>If (1, 4) is on the graph of f, then (4, 6) is on the graph of g</div><div>If f(x) = x², then g(x) = (x − 3)² + 2, with vertex (3, 2)</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>This is why vertex form from Class 9 works: <i>a</i>(<i>x</i> − <i>h</i>)² + <i>k</i> is the parabola <i>ax</i>² shifted right <i>h</i> and up <i>k</i>.</p>\n<p><b>Desmos tip.</b> Define <code>f(x)</code> first, then type <code>y=f(x-3)+2</code> on a new line to see the shift. Use sliders for <i>h</i> and <i>k</i> in <code>y=f(x-h)+k</code> to watch the graph move.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 6 · Polynomial graphs and zeros", "mins": "about 5 min", "html": "<p>Beyond quadratics, polynomials have more turns and more zeros (<i>x</i>-intercepts). Three facts answer most questions.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Zeros.</b> If (<i>x</i> − <i>a</i>) is a factor, then <i>a</i> is a zero: the graph meets the <i>x</i>-axis at <i>x</i> = <i>a</i>. It works in reverse too: an <i>x</i>-intercept at 4 means (<i>x</i> − 4) is a factor.</li>\n<li><b>Multiplicity.</b> If a factor is squared, like (<i>x</i> − <i>a</i>)², the graph touches the <i>x</i>-axis at <i>a</i> and turns back without crossing.</li>\n<li><b>End behavior.</b> Even-degree polynomials (<i>x</i>², <i>x</i>⁴) go the same direction at both ends. Odd-degree polynomials (<i>x</i>³, <i>x</i>⁵) go opposite directions. With a positive leading coefficient the right end goes up; with a negative one it goes down.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote><i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = (<i>x</i> − 2)²(<i>x</i> + 1)\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Zeros: x = 2 and x = −1</div><div>At x = −1 the graph crosses the axis; at x = 2 it touches and turns back</div><div>Degree 3, positive leading coefficient → left end down, right end up</div><div>y-intercept: f(0) = (−2)²(1) = 4</div></div></blockquote>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-mistake\"><b>Common mistake.</b> The sign flips between factor and zero, as in Class 9. The factor (<i>x</i> + 1) gives the zero −1, not 1.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 7 · Rational equations", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<p>A rational equation has a variable in a denominator. The simplest form is <i>P</i>(<i>x</i>) / <i>Q</i>(<i>x</i>) = 0.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>The rule.</b> A fraction is zero <b>only if the numerator is zero</b> and the denominator is not.</li>\n<li><b>Extraneous solutions.</b> Check whether each solution makes a denominator zero. If it does, discard it.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>(<i>x</i>² − 9) / (<i>x</i> − 3) = 0\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Numerator zero: x² − 9 = 0 → x = 3 or x = −3</div><div>x = 3 makes the denominator zero → discard</div><div>Answer: x = −3</div></div></blockquote>\n<p>When the equation has fractions on both sides, multiply every term by the denominators to clear them, solve, and run the same check.</p>\n<blockquote>6 / (<i>x</i> − 1) = 3\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Multiply by (x − 1): 6 = 3(x − 1) → x = 3</div><div>Check: the denominator is 3 − 1 = 2, not zero → x = 3</div></div></blockquote>"},
      {"title": "Part 8 · The Desmos angle", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<ul>\n<li><b>Polynomial zeros:</b> type the polynomial and click the <i>x</i>-intercepts. A point where the curve touches the axis and turns back is a repeated zero.</li>\n<li><b>Rational equations:</b> graph the left side and the right side as two expressions and read the <i>x</i>-coordinates of the intersections. A value that makes a denominator zero shows as a gap or a vertical break, never as an intersection.</li>\n<li><b>Function values:</b> define <code>f(x)=2x^2+5</code>, then type <code>f(4)</code>. Composite functions work the same way: <code>f(g(2))</code>.</li>\n</ul>"}
    ]
  },
  "U22": {
    lead: "<p>This is the question type built from student notes. You get a set of bullet-point notes on a topic, then a goal: “The student wants to emphasize the cost savings. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?” It is one of the most learnable types, because it depends less on reading skill and more on following a filter procedure exactly.</p>",
    parts: [
      {"title": "Part 1 · The core idea", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<p>The setup is always the same: a few bullet notes with facts, then a goal that asks for something specific: emphasize an advantage, introduce a topic, explain a drawback, make a comparison, respond to a concern.</p>\n<p>Two mechanical skills decide these questions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Goal-reading:</b> turn the goal into a filter. “Emphasize the cost savings” means the right answer must put cost in the foreground. Every choice that does not is out, however well written and accurate.</li>\n<li><b>Accuracy-checking:</b> every note is a fact you must not distort. Choices contain small misstatements: a number shifted, a “may” turned into “will”, a comparison reversed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The traps are predictable. One choice meets the goal but distorts a fact. Another is accurate but ignores the goal. A third is accurate and mentions the goal item without making it the point. The correct choice achieves the goal <b>and</b> uses the notes accurately.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 2 · The method", "mins": "about 6 min", "html": "<ol>\n<li><b>Read the goal twice and turn it into a requirement.</b> Write your own one-line version: “the sentence must lead with the speed advantage.”</li>\n<li><b>Check each choice for the goal first.</b> The order matters. Students who check accuracy first get distracted by a choice whose facts are flawless but whose sentence ignores the goal.</li>\n<li><b>Verify every fact in the survivors against the notes.</b> Check numbers, dates, names and the strength of claims. “Can generate up to 40% savings” must not become “generates 40% savings”; the “up to” has to survive. “More efficient than X” must not become plain “efficient” or “more efficient than Y”.</li>\n<li><b>Reject added information.</b> The notes are the complete fact base. If a choice states something no bullet contains, it is out, even if it sounds plausible and supports the goal.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-tip\"><b>Tip.</b> The goal verb is the answer's job description. Match the shape of the sentence to the job the verb describes.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 3 · The common goal types", "mins": "about 5 min", "html": "<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Goal</th><th>The right answer</th><th>Wrong choices</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Emphasize X</td><td>Puts X in the main clause, often first</td><td>Bury X in a side phrase (“while saving water, the garden thrives”) or omit it</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Introduce a topic to an unfamiliar audience</td><td>Defines the topic in plain terms and assumes nothing</td><td>Dive into details or use jargon the audience would not know</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Explain a drawback or concern</td><td>States the negative directly, with the reason</td><td>Lead with the benefit and hide the drawback</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Compare two items</td><td>Names both items and the direction (more than, less than, similar to)</td><td>Reverse the comparison or mention only one item</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Present information from a specific source</td><td>Matches that bullet exactly, including hedges like “researchers believe”</td><td>Turn belief into proof</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>"},
      {"title": "Part 4 · Worked example", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<blockquote><b>Notes</b>\n<ul>\n<li>Solar panels convert sunlight to electricity with no emissions during operation.</li>\n<li>A typical home system costs $15,000 before incentives; tax credits can reduce the cost by up to 30%.</li>\n<li>Panels typically lose about 0.5% of output per year.</li>\n<li>Installation takes one to three days for most homes.</li>\n</ul>\n<b>Goal.</b> The student wants to emphasize that solar panel costs can be reduced.</blockquote>\n<p><b>Requirement.</b> The sentence must make cost reduction its main point, not a side note.</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Choice</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>A) Solar panels produce electricity with no emissions during operation, and installation typically takes one to three days.</td><td>Accurate, but the goal is not addressed at all.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>B) Solar panels lose only about 0.5% of output per year, which, along with tax credits of up to 30%, makes them a lasting investment.</td><td>Mentions the tax credit in a side phrase. The main point is output, and “a lasting investment” is not in the notes.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>C) While a typical home solar system costs $15,000 before incentives, tax credits can reduce that cost by up to 30%.</b></td><td><b>Correct.</b> The main clause is the cost reduction, and “can” and “up to 30%” are preserved.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D) Tax credits eliminate the cost of solar panel installation entirely.</td><td>On the goal, but distorts the notes: up to 30%, not all of it.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>This is the typical pattern: A fails the goal, B mentions it without emphasizing it, D fails accuracy, C does both.</p>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-mistake\"><b>Common mistake.</b> Stopping the accuracy check at the numbers. Distortions hide in hedging words too: “can reduce” becoming “will reduce”, “up to 30%” becoming “30%”, “some researchers suggest” becoming “researchers have proven”. A trap choice keeps the numbers intact and changes the strength of the claim.</p>"}
    ]
  },
  "U23": {
    lead: "<p>Transition questions give you a short text with a blank and ask “Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?” The four choices are transition words. Your job is to pick the one whose logic fits the relationship between the two parts. These are fast points once you know the system, because every transition belongs to a small set of categories.</p>",
    parts: [
      {"title": "Part 1 · The core idea", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<p>A transition word is a logic signpost. It tells the reader how the next piece relates to the previous one. There are five signposts.</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Signpost</th><th>Words</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Same direction (addition)</td><td>moreover, furthermore, additionally, also, similarly, likewise, in addition</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Reverse direction (contrast)</td><td>however, nevertheless, nonetheless, still, in contrast, by contrast, on the other hand, instead</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cause and effect (result)</td><td>therefore, thus, consequently, accordingly, hence, as a result</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Example or restatement</td><td>for example, for instance, specifically, in fact, indeed, in other words</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sequence (time, order)</td><td>first, next, then, finally, subsequently, meanwhile, previously</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The whole question reduces to one thing: <b>what is the logical relationship between the two parts?</b> Decide that from their meaning. Then pick the category, then the word.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 2 · The method", "mins": "about 5 min", "html": "<ol>\n<li><b>Read the two parts as separate statements.</b> Cover the blank and state each side in plain words. “Side 1: the results were inconsistent. Side 2: the team repeated the experiment three times.” Is side 2 adding support, contrasting, or a consequence?</li>\n<li><b>Look for the logic already in the sentence.</b> “The results were disappointing; ___, the team remained optimistic” has a built-in contrast between <i>disappointing</i> and <i>optimistic</i>, so the blank needs a contrast word.</li>\n<li><b>Predict the category, then check the choices.</b> Say “this needs contrast” or “this needs cause and effect”, then find the choice in that family. Usually only one choice is from the right family.</li>\n<li><b>Plug it in.</b> Read the sentence with your word. If the signpost contradicts what the sentence does, the choice is wrong.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>When two choices are from the same family</b>, the difference is in how each is used:</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Contrast word</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>however</td><td>General contrast</td></tr>\n<tr><td>nevertheless, nonetheless, still</td><td>“Despite that”: side 2 happens in spite of side 1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>in contrast, by contrast</td><td>Two different things compared side by side</td></tr>\n<tr><td>instead</td><td>One thing replaces another</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote><b>Example.</b> The museum's attendance declined sharply after 2008. ______ the museum's revenue rose, because ticket prices increased.\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Declined vs. rose → opposite directions → contrast → However, / Nevertheless,</div></div></blockquote>"},
      {"title": "Part 3 · The two classic traps", "mins": "about 5 min", "html": "<p><b>Trap 1: contrast that is really a consequence.</b></p>\n<blockquote>The program was expensive; ______ it attracted few participants.</blockquote>\n<p>A contrast word sounds sophisticated here, but few participants is what you would expect from a high price. Side 2 goes the same way as side 1 and follows from it: “consequently” or “as a result”. Do not decide the logic from the topic. Decide it from whether side 2 goes along with side 1 or pushes against it.</p>\n<p><b>Trap 2: contrast inside a similarity.</b></p>\n<blockquote>The two species occupy similar habitats; ______ their hunting strategies differ.</blockquote>\n<p>Students pick “similarly” because the first clause says “similar”. But the clause after the blank is about a difference, so the category is contrast: “however”.</p>\n<p class=\"lw-note lw-tip\"><b>Tip.</b> The transition describes how the part <i>after</i> it relates to what came before. Students get caught matching the word to the clause before the blank. Always ask: how does the upcoming clause relate to the previous one?</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 4 · Worked examples", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<blockquote>Early electric cars had limited range. ______ modern electric vehicles can travel over 300 miles on a single charge.</blockquote>\n<p>Side 1: old electric cars had limited range. Side 2: modern ones go far. Opposite directions, and no causal link: range did not improve <i>because</i> early cars were limited.</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Choice</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>A) Similarly,</td><td>Both sentences are about range, but the directions are opposite.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>B) However,</b></td><td><b>Correct.</b> Contrast.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>C) Therefore,</td><td>Invents a cause.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D) For example,</td><td>Side 2 is not an example of side 1.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>The soil in the region is rich in minerals. ______ farming there yields exceptional harvests.</blockquote>\n<p>Side 1: rich soil. Side 2: great harvests. Side 2 is a consequence of side 1, since the soil explains the harvests. Category: result. “Therefore,” “Consequently,” or “As a result,”. “However” would claim the harvests happen despite the rich soil, which reverses the logic.</p>"}
    ]
  },
  "U24": {
    lead: "<p>This is the final content lesson. It builds on Class 20 and covers the harder half of the geometry domain: right triangles, trigonometry and circles. These are the geometry topics that appear most often in the hard second module.</p>",
    parts: [
      {"title": "Part 1 · Right triangles", "mins": "about 5 min", "html": "<p><b>Pythagorean theorem.</b> In a right triangle with legs <i>a</i> and <i>b</i> and hypotenuse <i>c</i>, <i>a</i>² + <i>b</i>² = <i>c</i>². It is on the reference sheet. The hypotenuse is always the side opposite the right angle, and it is the longest side.</p>\n<p>Common whole-number triples save time: 3-4-5, 5-12-13, 8-15-17, and their multiples such as 6-8-10.</p>\n<p><b>Special right triangles.</b> Know the ratios, and check them on the reference sheet.</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Triangle</th><th>Sides</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>45-45-90</td><td><i>x</i>, <i>x</i>, <i>x</i>√2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>30-60-90</td><td><i>x</i>, <i>x</i>√3, 2<i>x</i>, where <i>x</i> is opposite the 30° angle</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>"},
      {"title": "Part 2 · Trigonometry", "mins": "about 5 min", "html": "<p><b>Trigonometry.</b> SOH-CAH-TOA is the foundation.</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Ratio</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>sin θ</td><td>opposite / hypotenuse</td></tr>\n<tr><td>cos θ</td><td>adjacent / hypotenuse</td></tr>\n<tr><td>tan θ</td><td>opposite / adjacent</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Key property:</b> sin <i>x</i>° = cos (90 − <i>x</i>)°. Since sin 30° = 0.5, cos 60° is also 0.5. If a question says sin <i>a</i>° = cos <i>b</i>°, then <i>a</i> + <i>b</i> = 90.</p>"},
      {"title": "Part 3 · Circle equations", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<p><b>Circle equations.</b> The standard form is (<i>x</i> − <i>h</i>)² + (<i>y</i> − <i>k</i>)² = <i>r</i>², where (<i>h</i>, <i>k</i>) is the center and <i>r</i> is the radius.</p>\n<p>When the equation is not in that form, <b>complete the square</b> for both <i>x</i> and <i>y</i> (the method from Class 11). This is a common hard Module 2 question.</p>\n<blockquote><i>x</i>² + 6<i>x</i> + <i>y</i>² − 4<i>y</i> = 12\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>(x² + 6x + 9) + (y² − 4y + 4) = 12 + 9 + 4</div><div>(x + 3)² + (y − 2)² = 25</div><div>Center (−3, 2), radius 5</div></div></blockquote>"},
      {"title": "Part 4 · Arcs, sectors and radians", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<p><b>Arcs and sectors.</b> An arc or sector is a fraction of the whole circle, set by its central angle.</p>\n<blockquote><div class=\"lw-work\"><div>Arc length = (angle / 360) × 2πr</div><div>Sector area = (angle / 360) × πr²</div><div>Degrees to radians: multiply by π / 180</div></div></blockquote>"},
      {"title": "Part 5 · Circle theorems", "mins": "about 4 min", "html": "<p>Beyond the reference sheet, the SAT tests these four properties.</p>\n<div class=\"lw-table\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Theorem</th><th>What it says</th><th>How it is used</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Tangent–radius</td><td>A tangent line is perpendicular (90°) to the radius at the point of tangency.</td><td>It creates a right triangle, so the Pythagorean theorem applies.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Inscribed angle</td><td>An inscribed angle is half the central angle that intercepts the same arc.</td><td>A 100° central angle gives a 50° inscribed angle on the same arc.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tangents from one point</td><td>Two tangent segments from the same external point are equal in length.</td><td>Set the two lengths equal to find an unknown.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Diameter rule</td><td>A triangle inscribed in a circle with the diameter as one side is a right triangle.</td><td>The right angle is opposite the diameter, which is the hypotenuse.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote>A tangent from point P touches a circle of radius 5 at T. P is 13 from the center O. How long is PT?\n<div class=\"lw-work\"><div>The radius OT is perpendicular to PT → right triangle with hypotenuse OP</div><div>PT² = 13² − 5² = 144 → PT = 12</div></div></blockquote>"},
      {"title": "Part 6 · The Desmos application", "mins": "about 3 min", "html": "<ul>\n<li><b>Circle equations:</b> type (<i>x</i> − 2)² + (<i>y</i> + 3)² = 16 directly, or the expanded form without rearranging. Desmos draws the circle. Click the highest, lowest, leftmost and rightmost points: the center is midway between them, and half the width is the radius.</li>\n<li><b>Trigonometry:</b> to evaluate something like cos 45°, check the angle mode first (the wrench icon). Use Degrees if the question uses degrees and Radians if it uses radians.</li>\n</ul>"}
    ]
  }
};
